\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item {\textit{Unified Implementation of PQ-DNS Security Protocols:}} In this paper, we analyze the impact of the transition of DNS security protocols, in particular, DNSSEC, DoT, and DoH, to post-quantum cryptography. Our work fills the gap in the literature by implementing and experimentally evaluating three post-quantum secure DNS configurations: DNS-over-TLS with PQ-TLS, DNS-over-HTTPS with PQ-TLS, and DNSSEC with post-quantum digital signatures. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first empirical evaluation covering all three core DNS security extensions under standardized post-quantum cryptographic configurations.
    \item {\textit{Comprehensive Evaluation Framework:}} We provide a comprehensive performance and compatibility assessment using NIST-recommended algorithms, thereby contributing practical insights into the feasibility of DNS migration to a post-quantum cryptographic future. We begin with an overview of the current use of cryptographic primitives in each protocol and the vulnerabilities they face in a post-quantum era. We then evaluate candidate post-quantum algorithms for digital signatures and key exchange based on their applicability to DNS use cases, considering constraints such as message size, computational load, and protocol compatibility. 
