\section{Real Applications}
\label{sec:real}

\subsection{Riboflavin Dataset}

The \texttt{riboflavin} dataset from \citet{Schelldorfer2011} contains data on riboflavin production by Bacillus subtilis, a bacterium used in industrial fermentation. Besides a continuous response variable measuring the (log-transformed) production rate in bacterium samples, the dataset comprises $p=4,088$ predictor variables in the form of bacterium gene expression levels. The task is to discover the genes relevant to riboflavin production, with the ultimate goal of genetically modifying the bacterium to achieve a higher production rate. Towards this end, the dataset includes observations on $m=28$ bacterium samples, with each sample measured multiple times ($n_i\in\{2,\dots,6\}$), resulting in a total of $n=111$ observations.

We randomly split the dataset into training, validation, and testing sets in 0.70-0.15-0.15 proportions. Table~\ref{tab:riboflavin} reports the results from 100 such splits.
\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\small
\input{Tables/riboflavin.tex}
\caption{Comparisons on the \texttt{riboflavin} dataset. The averages and standard errors (parentheses) are measured over 100 splits of the data. Prediction error is the squared-error loss relative to the fixed intercept-only model.}
\label{tab:riboflavin}
\end{table}
The LMMs fit by \texttt{glmmsel} achieve the lowest prediction loss and the second lowest sparsity level on average. \texttt{L0Learn} yields LMs that are slightly sparser, but because they cannot capture heterogeneity across samples, they exhibit worse prediction loss. Meanwhile, the LMs fit by \texttt{glmnet} exhibit statistically comparable prediction loss to the LMMs by \texttt{glmmsel} but contain more than twice as many genes on average, possibly to compensate for their inability to incorporate heterogeneous effects. The other sparse LMM methods considered in the previous experiments (\texttt{pysr3}, \texttt{glmmPen}, and \texttt{rpql}) are unable to scale to the $p=4,088$ predictors in this dataset. Indeed, \texttt{glmmsel} is the only such method capable of learning LMMs on datasets of this size.

\subsection{Mashable Dataset}

The \texttt{mashable} dataset from \citet{Fernandes2015} contains data on the popularity of news articles posted to the online news platform Mashable. In addition to a binary response variable indicating whether an article is popular (classified as achieving more than the median number of social media shares), the dataset comprises $p=53$ predictor variables that characterize the article (e.g., number of total words, positive words, and images). The task is to identify the article characteristics that are reliable determinants of popularity. To that end, the dataset includes observations over $m=27$ days in December 2014, each involving varying numbers of articles ($n_i\in\{20,\dots,100\}$), giving a total of $n=1,700$ observations.

We randomly split the dataset into training, validation, and testing sets in the same proportions as before. Table~\ref{tab:mashable} reports the results from 100 of these splits.
