\texttt{glmmsel} again stands out by producing GLMMs that attain the lowest prediction loss and contain the fewest predictors (approximately 14 article characteristics on average). In contrast, the GLMs from \texttt{L0Learn} use roughly 30\% more article characteristics and rely solely on fixed effects, leading to worse prediction loss overall. The prediction loss and sparsity of \texttt{glmnet} is worse still, the latter metric being 50\% larger than that of \texttt{glmmsel}. At the same time, the other sparse GLMM methods currently available for binary response data (\texttt{glmmPen} and \texttt{rpql}) fail to run or converge on this dataset. Indeed, similar to the \texttt{riboflavin} dataset, \texttt{glmmsel} is the only method that is capable of successfully learning sparse GLMMs on the \texttt{mashable} dataset.

\section{Concluding Remarks}
\label{sec:concluding}

LMMs are key tools for modeling heterogeneous patterns encountered in contemporary data-analytic problems, such as personalized medicine or adaptive marketing. These problems increasingly involve datasets with a high dimensionality of predictors, making sparsity a desirable (even necessary) quality from the standpoints of prediction and interpretation. This paper introduces a new $\ell_0$ regularized estimator for subset selection in LMMs. We demonstrate that coordinate descent, coupled with a low-complexity local search routine, can scale our estimator to wide datasets well beyond the reach of existing methods. These algorithms also readily extend to subset selection in GLMMs via a PQL approximation. Besides scalability, the new estimator is shown to have excellent statistical properties in theory, simulation, and practice. Our \texttt{R} package \texttt{glmmsel} is available on \texttt{CRAN} and \texttt{GitHub}.

