\section{Introduction}
Heisenberg inequality \cite{heisenberg,weyl,kennard} was first proposed by the German physicist Heisenberg in 1927, which indicates that in a quantum system, the position and momentum of a particle cannot be precisely measured simultaneously. In 1928, Weyl and Pauli demonstrated that the energy of a signal and that of its Fourier transform (FT) also satisfy the Heisenberg inequality. The FT \cite{FT,FT2,FT3} is one of the most significant analytical tools in applied mathematics, enabling the transformation of signals from the time domain to the frequency domain. Consequently, the Heisenberg inequality reveals an inherent limitation in the simultaneous concentration of energy in both the time and frequency domains \cite{HPW}, which is commonly recognized as the Heisenberg uncertainty principle in signal processing. This principle states that the time duration $\Delta_{f(t)}$ of a signal $f(t)$ and the frequency bandwidth $\Delta_{\hat{f}(\xi)}$ of its FT $\hat{f}(\xi)$ are constrained by the following inequality \cite{UN}
\begin{equation}
\Delta_{f(t)} \Delta_{\hat{f}(\xi)} \ge \frac{1}{2},
\end{equation} 
where 
\begin{equation}
\label{81}
\Delta_{f(t)} = \left[\frac{1}{||f(t)||^2} \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \left(t-\alpha_t \right)^2 \left|f(t) \right|^2 dt \right]^{\frac{1}{2}},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\Delta_{\hat{f}(\xi)}= \left[\frac{1}{||\hat{f}(\xi)||^2} \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \left(\xi-\alpha_{\xi} \right)^2 \left|\hat{f}(\xi) \right|^2 d\xi\right]^{\frac{1}{2}},
\end{equation}
and $\alpha_t$ and $\alpha_{\xi}$ are defined as
\begin{equation}
\label{82}
\alpha_t = \frac{1}{||f(t) ||^2}\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} t \left|f(t) \right|^2 dt, 
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\alpha_{\xi}= \frac{1}{||\hat{f}(\xi) ||^2}\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \xi \left|\hat{f}(\xi) \right|^2 d\xi.
\end{equation}
This uncertainty principle conveys two valuable insights \cite{xu}. First, the resolutions in the time and frequency domains cannot be simultaneously increased without bound, as their product is constrained by a nonzero lower limit. Second, there exists a trade-off relationship between time and frequency resolution, meaning that improving frequency resolution inevitably leads to a reduction in time resolution, and vice versa. Time resolution and frequency resolution are key parameters in signal detection. High time resolution facilitates precise localization, while high frequency resolution enables accurate estimation of spectral components, such as velocity or modulation characteristics. Therefore, the uncertainty principle has become one of the important topics in harmonic analysis \cite{UPDP, UP1, UPR, UP2, UP3, UP4}. 

Building on the above research, John Michael Rassias proposed the generalized Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle in 2004 \cite{HPW}, which states that for $f \in L^2(\mathbb{R})$ and any fixed but arbitrary $p \in \mathbb{N}$, there holds
\begin{equation}
%\label{60}
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt[2p]{(\mu_{2p})_{\omega,\lvert f \rvert^2}} \sqrt[2p]{(\mu_{2p})_{\lvert \hat{f} \rvert}} \geq \frac{1}{2 \pi \sqrt[p]{2}} \sqrt[p]{\lvert E_{p,f} \rvert}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
In the following year, he presented the Heisenberg-Weyl uncertainty principle \cite{HW}, which can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
(\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| f \right|^2}^{1/ p} (\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| \hat{f} \right|^2}^{1/ p} \ge E_{\left| f \right|^2}^{2/ p} /4 \pi.
\end{equation}
To obtain a more precise bound for practical applications, Rassias proposed a sharpened version of the Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle in the Fourier analysis in 2006 \cite{SHW}, which is stated as follows
\begin{equation}
\label{2}
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt[2p]{(\mu_{2p})_{\omega,\lvert f \rvert^2}} \sqrt[2p]{(\mu_{2p})_{\lvert \hat{f} \rvert}} \geq \frac{1}{2 \pi \sqrt[p]{2}} \sqrt[p]{\lvert E_{p,f}^{*} \rvert^2}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The above uncertainty principles are all established based on the FT \cite{FT,FT2,FT3}, which can convert time domain signals into the frequency domain to facilitate the analysis of their frequency characteristics. However, for non-stationary signals, the FT falls short in providing optimal time-frequency resolution, as it cannot effectively capture the temporal variations of the spectrum. To address this limitation, a range of advanced time-frequency analysis tools such as the fractional Fourier transform (FrFT) \cite{FrFt, FrFt2, FrFt3}, the linear canonical transform (LCT) \cite{LCT, LCT2} and the offset linear canonical transform (OLCT) have been developed. 

The OLCT is the generalization of the FT, FrFT and LCT \cite{OLCT, OLCTtwo}. It has six parameters $(a, b, c, d, \tau, \eta)$, which makes it more flexible. Mathematically, the OLCT of a signal $f(t)$ with parameter
$\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{0.8}
J = \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}
a&b&\tau\\
c&d&\eta\\
\end{array}\right]$ is defined as \cite{OLCT}
\begin{equation}
\label{1}
\begin{aligned}
O_Jf(\xi)=O_J[f(t)](\xi)
=\begin{cases}
\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} f(t) K_J(t,\xi)dt,b \neq 0\\
\sqrt{d} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{j} [\frac{cd(\xi-\tau)^2}{2}+\xi \eta]} f(d\xi-d\tau),b=0
\end{cases}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
In recent years, various uncertainty principles based on the FrFT \cite{FrFtUN, FrFtUN2, FrFtDP}, LCT \cite{LCTUN, DB} and OLCT \cite{OLCTUN1, OLCTUN2, OLCTUN3} have been extensively investigated. However, the $2p$ order Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principles in the OLCT domain remain unexplored, highlighting the need for further research in this area. The OLCT has demonstrated considerable theoretical and practical potential in applications such as time-frequency analysis, filter design, and target detection \cite{OLCTthree}. Therefore, studying the uncertainty principle associated with the OLCT is of both theoretical and practical importance.

In this paper, three types of the Heisenberg uncertainty principles related to the OLCT are proposed. In section \ref{sec3}, we extend the Plancherel-Parseval-Rayleigh identity,  the $2p$ order Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle, the Heisenberg-Weyl uncertainty principle and the sharpened Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle in OLCT domain. Section \ref{sec4} gives experimental validations of the proposed theoretical results. Section \ref{sec5} concludes this paper.

\section{Main Results}
\label{sec3}
This section derives the main theorems of this paper, including the Plancherel-Parseval-Rayleigh identity, the $2p$ order Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle, the Heisenberg-Weyl uncertainty principle, and the sharpened Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle in the OLCT domain.
\subsection{The Plancherel-Parseval-Rayleigh identity of the OLCT}
To derive the $2p$ order Heisenberg uncertainty principles in the OLCT domain, we first derived the Plancherel-Parseval-Rayleigh identity, which can be represented as the following Theorem \ref{thm3.1}.
\begin{thm}
	\label{thm3.1}
	Suppose that $f(t)$, $O_f^J(\xi)$, $(\xi-\xi_m)^p O_f^J(\xi)$ belong to $L^1(\mathbb{R}) \cap L^2(\mathbb{R})$ and $p \in \mathbb{N} = \{0,1,2,...\}$. Then we have
	\begin{equation}
	\label{14}
	\int_{\mathbb{R}} (\xi-\xi_m)^{2p} \lvert O_f^J(\xi) \rvert^2 d \xi = b^{2p} \int_{\mathbb{R}} \lvert g_\beta^{(p)}(t) \rvert^2 dt,
	\end{equation}
	where $g_{\beta}^{(p)}(t) = \frac{d^p}{dt^p} \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{j} \beta t} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{j} \frac{a}{2b} t^2}  f(t)$ with $\beta=2 \pi \sigma_m$, $\sigma_m = \frac{\xi_m-\tau}{2 \pi b}$. %and $\sigma = \frac{\xi-\tau}{2 \pi b}$.
\end{thm}

\begin{proof}
	Regarding the left-hand side of \eqref{14}, it can be rewritten as
	\begin{align*}
	&\int_{\mathbb{R}} (\xi-\xi_m)^{2p} \lvert O_f^J(\xi) \rvert^2 d \xi \nonumber\\
	=& \int_{\mathbb{R}} (\xi-\xi_m)^{2p} \left| \frac{1}{\sqrt{\mathrm{j} 2\pi b}} \int_{\mathbb{R}} f(t) \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{j}[\frac{a}{2b} t^2-\frac{1}{b}t(\xi-\tau)-\frac{1}{b}\xi(d\tau -b \eta)+\frac{d}{2b}(\xi^2+\tau^2)]} dt \right|^2 d \xi \nonumber\\
	=&\frac{1}{2 \pi b} \int_{\mathbb{R}}(\xi-\xi_m)^{2p} \left| \int_{\mathbb{R}} f(t) \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{j} [\frac{a}{2b} t^2-\frac{(\xi-\tau)t}{b}]}dt \right|^2 d\xi.
	\end{align*}
	Since $g(t) = f(t)\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{j} \frac{a}{2b} t^2}$, then
	\begin{align}
	\label{16}
	&\int_{\mathbb{R}} (\xi-\xi_m)^{2p} \left| O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2 d \xi \nonumber\\
	=&\frac{1}{2 \pi b} \int_{\mathbb{R}}(\xi-\xi_m)^{2p} \left| \int_{\mathbb{R}} g(t) \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{j} \frac{(\xi-\tau)}{b}t}dt \right|^2 d \xi \nonumber\\
	=&\frac{1}{2 \pi b} \int_{\mathbb{R}}(\xi-\xi_m)^{2p} \left| \hat{g}(\frac{\xi-\tau}{2 \pi b})\right|^2 d \xi \nonumber\\
	=&\frac{1}{2 \pi b} \int_{\mathbb{R}} [\xi-\tau-(\xi_m-\tau)]^{2p} \left| \hat{g}(\frac{\xi-\tau}{2 \pi b}) \right|^2 d( \xi-\tau) \nonumber \\
	=&\frac{1}{2 \pi b} \int_{\mathbb{R}} (2 \pi b)^{2p} (\sigma-\sigma_m)^{2p} \left| \hat{g}(\sigma) \right|^2 d(2 \pi b \sigma) \nonumber\\
	=&(2 \pi b)^{2p} \int_{\mathbb{R}} (\sigma-\sigma_m)^{2p} \left| \hat{g}(\sigma) \right|^2 d\sigma.
	\end{align}
	The following identity can be derived based on the result presented in \cite{HPW}.
	\begin{equation}
	\label{76}
	\int_{\mathbb{R}} (\sigma-\sigma_m)^{2p} \left| \hat{g}(\sigma) \right|^2 d\sigma = \frac{1}{(2\pi )^{2p}} \int_{\mathbb{R}} \lvert g_{\beta}^{(p)}(t) \rvert^2 dt.
	\end{equation}
	Substituting \eqref{76} into \eqref{16} completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The $2p$ order Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle of the OLCT}
We define
\begin{equation}
(\mu_{2p})_{\omega,\left|f(t) \right|^2}= \int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega^2(x) (t-t_m)^{2p} \left|f(t) \right|^2 dt,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\label{53}
(\mu_{2p})_{\left| O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2} = \int_{\mathbb{R}} (\xi-\xi_m)^{2p} \left|O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2 d\xi,
\end{equation}
to establish the $2p$ order Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle in the OLCT domain as following Theorem \ref{thm3.2}. 
\begin{thm}
	\label{thm3.2}
	Suppose that $f(t)$, $O_f^J(\xi)$, $(\xi-\xi_m)^p O_f^J(\xi)$ belong to $L^1(\mathbb{R}) \cap L^2(\mathbb{R})$. Let $\omega(t)$ be a real weight function. Then the $2p$ order Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle in the OLCT domain is as follows
	\begin{equation}
	\label{17}
	(\mu_{2p})_{\omega,\left|f(t) \right|^2}(\mu_{2p})_{\left| O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2} \ge \frac{b^{2p}}{4} (E_{p,f})^2,
	\end{equation}
	where 
	\begin{equation}
	\label{75}
	E_{p,f}=\sum_{q=0}^{\left[ \frac{p}{2} \right]} D_q F_q,
	\end{equation}
	\begin{equation*}
	D_q = (-1)^q \frac{p}{p-q}\binom{p-q}{q}, F_q = \sum_{n=0}^q B_{qn} I_{qn} +2\sum_{0 \leq i < z \leq q} C_{qiz} I_{qiz}.
	\end{equation*} 
	Here
	\begin{equation*}
B_{qn}= \binom{q}{n}^2 \beta^{2(q-n)}, C_{qiz} = s_{qi} \binom{q}{i} \binom{q}{z} \beta^{2q-z-i}, s_{qi} = (-1)^{q-i} \in \{\pm1,\pm\mathrm{j}\},
	\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
I_{qn} = (-1)^{q-2p} \int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega_p^{(p-2q)} (t) \left| f^{(n)} (t) \right|^2 dt, 
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
I_{qiz} = (-1)^{q-2p} \int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega_p^{(p-2q)} (t) \mathrm{Re} \left((-1)^{q - \frac{i + z}{2}}  f^{(i)}(t) \overline{f^{(z)} (t)} \right) dt. 
\end{equation*}
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
	Set
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
	\label{21}
	M_p=&(\mu_{2p})_{\omega,\left|f(t) \right|^2}(\mu_{2p})_{\left| O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2} \\
	=& \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega^2(t) (t-t_m)^{2p} \left|f(t) \right|^2 dt \right) \cdot \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} (\xi-\xi_m)^{2p} \left|O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2 d\xi \right). 
	\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	From \eqref{14} and \eqref{21}, we obtain
	\begin{align}
	\label{22}
	M_p=&b^{2p} \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega^2(x) (t-t_m)^{2p} \left| g_{\beta}(t) \right|^2 dt \right) \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left| g_{\beta}^{(p)} (t) \right| ^2 dt \right). 
	\end{align}
	Based on \eqref{22} and applying the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we get
	\begin{equation}
	\label{23}
	M_p \ge b^{2p} \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left| \omega_p(t) g_{\beta}(t) g_{\beta}^{(p)} (t) \right| dt \right)^2,
	\end{equation}
	where $\omega_p(t)=(t-t_m)^p \omega(t)$.
	Applying the complex inequality to \eqref{23}, we have
	\begin{equation}
	\label{25}
	M_p \ge \frac{b^{2p}}{4} \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega_p(t) \left( g_{\beta}(t) \overline{g_{\beta}^{(p)} (t)} + \overline{g_{\beta}(t)} g_{\beta}^{(p)} (t) \right) dt \right)^2.
	\end{equation}
	Using the differential identity on \eqref{25}, we obtain
	\begin{equation}
	\label{26}
	M_p \ge \frac{b^{2p}}{4} \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega_p(t) \left( \sum_{q=0}^{\left[ \frac{p}{2} \right]} D_q \frac{d^{p-2q}}{dt^{p-2q}} \left| g_{\beta}^{(q)} (t) \right|^2 \right)dt \right)^2.
	\end{equation}
	Applying the Lagrange type differential identity \cite{HPW} to $g_{\beta}^{(q)} (t)$, we get
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
	\label{27}
	g_{\beta}^{(q)} (t) = \sum_{n=0}^q B_{qn} \left|g^{(n)}(t) \right|^2 +2 \sum_{0 \leq i< z \leq q} C_{qiz} \mathrm{Re}((-1)^{q-\frac{i+z}{2}} g^{(i)}(t) g^{\overline{(z)}}(t)).
	\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	From the integral identity and \eqref{27}, we find
	\begin{equation}
	\label{28}
	\int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega_p(t) \frac{d^{p-2q}}{dt^{p-2q}} \left|g^{(n)}(t) \right|^2 dt = (-1)^{p-2q} \int_{\mathbb{R}}\omega_p^{p-2q}(t) \left|g^{(n)}(t) \right|^2 dt = I_{qn},
	%\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
	\label{29}
	&\int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega_p(t) \frac{d^{p-2q}}{dt^{p-2q}} \mathrm{Re}((-1)^{q-\frac{i+z}{2}} g^{(i)}(t) g^{\overline{(z)}}(t)) dt \\
	= &(-1)^{p-2q} \int_{\mathbb{R}}\omega_p^{p-2q}(t) \mathrm{Re}((-1)^{q-\frac{i+z}{2}} g^{(i)}(t) g^{\overline{(z)}}(t)) dt \\
	= &I_{qiz}.
	\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	Taking \eqref{28} and \eqref{29} into \eqref{26}, we obtain
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
	\label{80}
	M_p &\ge \frac{b^{2p}}{4} \left( \sum_{q=0}^{\left[ \frac{p}{2} \right]} D_q \left( \sum_{l=0}^q B_{qn} I_{qn} +2 \sum_{0 \leq i< z \leq q} C_{qiz} I_{qiz} \right) \right)^2 \\
	&=\frac{b^{2p}}{4} \left( E_{p,f} \right)^2.
	\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	This completes the proof of Theorem \ref{thm3.2}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
	\label{co1}
	For $p=1$, the second-order Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle in the OLCT domain can be obtained as follows
	\begin{equation}
	\label{70}
	(\mu_{2})_{\omega,\left|f(t) \right|^2}(\mu_{2})_{\left| O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2} \ge \frac{b^{2}}{4} (E_{1,f})^2,
	\end{equation}
	where
	\begin{equation}
	E_{1,f} = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left[ \left(t-t_m \right) \omega(t) \right]^{'} \left|f(t)\right|^2 dt.
	\end{equation}
	Equality holds in \eqref{70} if and only if
	\begin{equation}
	\label{72}
	f(t) = c_0 \mathrm{e}^{-c_p(t-t_m)^2}\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{j}(\frac{\xi_m}{b}t-\frac{a}{2b}t^2)},
	\end{equation}
	where $c_0$, $x_m$, $\xi_m \in \mathbb{R}$ and $c_p \in \mathbb{R}^+$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
	Assuming $\omega = 1$ and $ ||f||_2^2 = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left| f(t) \right|^2 dt = E_{\left|f \right|^2}$, the second-order Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle in the OLCT domain can be obtained as follows
	\begin{equation}
	\label{86}
	(\mu_{2})_{1,\left|f(t) \right|^2}(\mu_{2})_{\left| O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2} \ge \frac{b^{2}}{4} ||f||_2^4.
	\end{equation}
	When $ ||f||_2 = 1$, equation \eqref{86} reduces to the Heisenberg uncertainty principle derived in \cite{OLCTUN0}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
	When the parameters of the OLCT are set to $\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{0.8}
	J = \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}
	a&b&0\\
	c&d&0\\
	\end{array}\right]$, Theorem \ref{thm3.2} reduces to the $2p$ order uncertainty principle in the LCT domain, which is consistent with the result established in \cite{LCTUN}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
	When the parameters of the OLCT are set to $\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{0.8}
	J = \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}
	cos(\alpha)&sin(\alpha)&0\\
	-sin(\alpha)&cos(\alpha)&0\\
	\end{array}\right]$, Theorem \ref{thm3.2} reduces to the $2p$ order uncertainty principle in the FrFT domain, which is also derived in \cite{LCTUN}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
	When the parameters of the OLCT are set to $\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{0.8}
	J = \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}
	0&1&0\\
	-1&0&0\\
	\end{array}\right]$, Theorem \ref{thm3.2} reduces to the $2p$ order uncertainty principle in the FT domain, which is originally presented in \cite{HPW}.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The Heisenberg-Weyl uncertainty principle of the OLCT}
Let
\begin{equation}
(\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| f \right|^2}=\int_{\mathbb{R}} \left| t-t_m \right|^{p} \left| f(t) \right|^2 dt,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\label{57}
(\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2} = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left| \xi - \xi_m \right|^{p} \left| O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2 d \xi,
\end{equation}
then we can obtain the following Theorem \ref{thm3.3}.
\begin{thm}
	\label{thm3.3}
	Suppose that $f(t)$, $O_f^J(\xi)$, $(\xi-\xi_m)^p O_f^J(\xi)$ belong to $L^1(\mathbb{R}) \cap L^2(\mathbb{R})$. For $p \ge 2$ and any fixed but arbitrary real constants $t_m$, $\xi_m$, the Heisenberg-Weyl uncertainty principle of the OLCT holds as follows
	\begin{equation}
	\label{55}
	(\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| f \right|^2}^{1/ p} (\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2}^{1/ p} \ge \frac{b}{2} E_{\left| f \right|^2}^{\frac{2}{p}},
	\end{equation}
	where $E_{\left| f \right|^2} = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left| f(t) \right|^{2} dt$.
\end{thm}

\begin{proof}
	Firstly,
	\begin{equation}
	\label{83}
	\begin{aligned}
	(\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| f \right|^2}^{\frac{2}{p} }E_{\left| f \right|^2}^{1-\frac{2}{p}} &= \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left| t-t_m \right|^{p} \left| f(t) \right|^2 dt \right)^{\frac{2}{p}} \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left|f(t) \right|^2 dt \right)^{1-\frac{2}{p}}\\
	&= \left[\int_{\mathbb{R}} \left( \left| t-t_m \right|^{2} \left| f(t) \right|^{\frac{4}{p}} \right) ^{\frac{p}{2}}dt \right]^{\frac{2}{p}} \left[\int_{\mathbb{R}} \left( \left|f(t) \right|^{2\left(1-\frac{2}{p} \right)} \right)^{1/\left(1-\frac{2}{p} \right)}dt \right]^{1-\frac{2}{p}}.
	\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	Then applying the Hölder inequality to \eqref{83}, we obtain
	\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
	(\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| f \right|^2}^{\frac{2}{p} }E_{\left| f \right|^2}^{1-\frac{2}{p}} & \ge \int_{\mathbb{R}}  \left( (t-t_m) ^2 \left|f(t) \right|^{\frac{4}{p}} \right) \left(\left|f(t) \right|^{2\left(1-\frac{2}{p} \right) } \right)  dt\\
	&= \int_{\mathbb{R}} (t-t_m) ^2 \left|f(t) \right|^2 dt\\
	&= (\mu_{2}^{*})_{\left| f \right|^2} \triangleq \phi_{\left|f \right|^2}^2,
	\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	so 
	\begin{equation}
	\label{61}
	(\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| f \right|^2}^{\frac{1}{p}} \ge  \phi_{\left|f \right|^2} / E_{\left| f \right|^2}^{\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{p}}.
	\end{equation}
	Equality holds in \eqref{61} if and only if
	\begin{equation*}
	\left| t-t_m \right|^{p} E_{\left| f \right|^2} = (\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| f \right|^2}.
	\end{equation*}
	Similarly, 
	\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
	(\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2}^{\frac{2}{p} } E_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2}^{1-\frac{2}{p}} &= \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left| \xi-\xi_m \right|^{p} \left| O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2 d \xi \right)^{\frac{2}{p}} \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left| O_f^J (\xi) \right|^2 d\xi \right)^{1-\frac{2}{p}} \\
	&\ge \int_{\mathbb{R}} (\xi-\xi_m) ^2 \left| O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2 d\xi\\
	&=(\mu_{2}^{*})_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2} \triangleq \phi_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2}^2,
	\end{aligned}	
	\end{equation*}
	so 
	\begin{equation}
	\label{62}
	(\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2}^{\frac{1}{p}} \ge  \phi_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2} / E_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2}^{\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{p}}.
	\end{equation}
	Equality holds in \eqref{62} if and only if
	\begin{equation*}
	\left| \xi-\xi_m \right|^{p} E_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2} = (\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2}.
	\end{equation*}
	Based on the Parseval property of the OLCT \cite{OLCTP}, we obtain
	\begin{equation}
	E_{\left| f \right|^2} = E_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2},
	\end{equation}
	then we find
	\begin{equation}
	M_{p}^* = (\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| f \right|^2}^{\frac{1}{p}} (\mu_{p}^{*})_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2}^{\frac{1}{p}} \ge \phi_{\left|f \right|^2} \phi_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2} / E_{\left| f \right|^2}^{1-\frac{2}{p}}.
	\end{equation}
	From \eqref{86}, we have
	\begin{equation}
	\phi_{\left|f \right|^2}^2 \phi_{\left| O_f^J \right|^2}^2 \ge \frac{b^2}{4} E_{\left| f \right|^2} ^2.
	\end{equation}
	Theorem \ref{thm3.3} has been proven.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The sharpened Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle  of the OLCT}
This section builds upon the $2p$ order Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle in the OLCT domain and presents the sharpened Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle, which provides a more precise lower bound.
\begin{thm}
	\label{thm3.4}
Suppose that $f(t)$, $O_f^J(\xi)$, $(\xi-\xi_m)^p O_f^J(\xi)$ belong to $L^1(\mathbb{R}) \cap L^2(\mathbb{R})$. For any fixed but arbitrary $p \in \mathbb{N}$, the sharpened Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle of the OLCT holds as follows
	\begin{equation}
	\label{31}
	\sqrt[2p]{(\mu_{2p})_{\omega, \left| f \right|^2}} \sqrt[2p]{(\mu_{2p})_{\omega, \left| O_f^J \right|^2}} \ge \frac{b}{\sqrt[p]{2}} \sqrt[p]{\left| E_{p,f}^* \right|}.
	\end{equation}
	The equality in \eqref{31} holds when 
	\begin{equation}
	A = ||u(t)||x_0-||v(t)||y_0,
	\end{equation}
	where $u(t) = \omega(t) (t-t_m)^p g_{\beta}(t)$, $v(t) = g_{\beta}^{(p)} (t)$ and $x_0 = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left|v(t) \right| \left| h(t) \right| dt$, $y_0 = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left|u(t) \right| \left| h(t) \right| dt$, $\left\|h(t)\right\|^2 = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left|h(t) \right|^2 dt =1$.
\end{thm}

\begin{proof}
	In fact, from \eqref{14} in Theorem \ref{thm3.1}, we obtain
	\begin{align}
	\label{32}
	M_p^* =& M_p -b^{2p} A^2 \nonumber\\
	=&(\mu_{2p})_{\omega,\left|f(t) \right|^2}(\mu_{2p})_{\left| O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2} - b^{2p} A^2 \nonumber\\
	=&\left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega^2(t) (t-t_m)^{2p} \left|f(t) \right|^2 dt \right) \cdot \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} (\xi-\xi_m)^{2p} \left|O_f^J(\xi) \right|^2 d\xi \right) -b^{2p} A^2 \nonumber\\
	=&b^{2p} \left( \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \omega^2(t) (t-t_m)^{2p} \left| g_{\beta}(t) \right|^2 dt \right) \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left| g_{\beta}^{(p)} (t) \right| ^2 dt \right) -A^2 \right)  \nonumber\\
	=&b^{2p} \left( \left\|u\right\|^2 \left\|v\right\|^2 -A^2 \right).
	\end{align} 
	From the positive definiteness of the following Gram determinant, we obtain
	\begin{align*}
	0 \leq &\begin{pmatrix}
	\left\|u\right\|^2&(\left|u\right|,\left|v\right|)&y_0\\
	(\left|u\right|,\left|v\right|)&\left\|v\right\|^2&x_0\\
	y_0&x_0&1\\
	\end{pmatrix} \nonumber \\
	=&\left\|u\right\|^2 \left\|v\right\|^2-(\left|u\right|,\left|v\right|)-\left[\left\|u\right\|^2 x_0^2 -2(\left|u\right|,\left|v\right|)x_0y_0+\left\|v\right\|^2y_0^2 \right] \nonumber \\
	\leq &\left\|u\right\|^2 \left\|v\right\|^2-(\left|u\right|,\left|v\right|)-A^2,
	\end{align*}
	then we find
	\begin{align}
	\label{34}
	M_p^* \ge& b^{2p}(\left|u \right|,\left|v \right|)^2 \nonumber \\
	=&b^{2p} \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left|u \right| \left|v \right| \right)^2 \nonumber\\
	=&b^{2p}  \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}} \left|\omega_p(t) g_{\beta}(t) g_{\beta}^{(p)} (t) \right| dt \right)^2.
	\end{align}
	Equations \eqref{23} and \eqref{34} are exactly the same, so
	\begin{equation*}
	\label{35}
	M_p^* \ge \frac{b^{2p}}{4} \left( E_{p,f} \right)^2.
	\end{equation*}
	Using \eqref{32}, we have
	\begin{align}
	\label{36}
	M_p \ge& \frac{b^{2p}}{4} \left( E_{p,f} \right)^2+b^{2p} A^2 \nonumber\\
	=&\frac{b^{2p}}{4} \left( \left(E_{p,f} \right)^2+4A^2 \right).
	\end{align}
	So the $2p$ order sharpened Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle  of the OLCT is as follows
	\begin{equation*}
	\sqrt[2p]{M_p} \ge \frac{b}{\sqrt[p]{2}} \sqrt[p]{\left| E_{p,f}^{*} \right|},
	\end{equation*}
	where $\left| E_{p,f}^{*} \right| = \sqrt{E_{p,f}^2+4A^2}$.
	Hence, Theorem \ref{thm3.4} is proved.
\end{proof}

Theorem \ref{thm3.2} and Theorem \ref{thm3.4} provide two lower bounds for the Heisenberg-Pauli-Weyl uncertainty principle of the OLCT. From a mathematical standpoint, a larger value on the right side of the inequality indicates a more precise and reliable estimate of the left side. Therefore, it is crucial to study the sharpened version of the uncertainty principle. From a practical signal processing perspective, a smaller lower bound implies that higher time-frequency concentration is theoretically permissible in time-frequency analysis, thereby characterizing the ideal theoretical limit. However, it remains necessary to derive sharpened version of the uncertainty principle in the OLCT domain with larger lower bounds. The reason is that a smaller theoretical bound merely reflects the potential limit under ideal conditions, whereas actual signals often fail to attain this level of concentration. In contrast, sharpened lower bounds, although numerically larger, provide more precise estimates that are closer to the true achievable limits. 
