These highly skewed distributions among different circuits pose a great challenge to develop generic transfer learning methods. 
In regression task, we keep the coupling capacitor with capacitance $y_i \in [1^{-21}, 1^{-15}]$. 
In classification task, we divide the ground capacitors connecting to net nodes into 5 categories in terms of the magnitude of their capacitance values.

\vspace{-2pt}
\subsection{Edge Regression - Parasitic Capacitance Estimation}
\label{sec:com_reg}
In this task, we configure encoders in CircuitGCL with 4-layer ClusterGCN \cite{chiang2019cluster}, 256 hidden dimensions, Tanh activation, 0.3 dropout, and $1^{-6}$ learning rate. The downstream GNN adopts a 5-layer GraphSAGE \cite{hamilton2017inductive}, with 144 hidden dimensions, PReLU activation, 0.3 dropout, and $1^{-4}$ learning rate. The task-specific heads are 2-layer MLPs with the same number of hidden dimensions as the CL encoders and the downstream GNN, respectively. The $\sigma_{noise}$ in \autoref{eq:gmm_loss_multi} and \autoref{eq:bmc_loss} is set to be 0.001.
% Here we list the results of all existing methods and CircuitGCL, as shown in \autoref{tab:reg_comp}. Compared to the SOTA, CircuitGCL achieves the best results across all test sets. We highlight that introducing either GCL or label rebalancing techniques can improve the model's performance on specific test circuits without sacrificing the accuracy of other test sets.

Table \ref{tab:reg_comp} compares CircuitGCL against prior methods on parasitic capacitance regression across four test sets.   
The GAI variant of balances MSE achieves the best overall performance, with the lowest MAE (0.0610–0.0935) and MSE (0.0089–0.0187) across all test sets. 
CircuitGCL variants show robust scalability: on the largest test set (\sandwich), GAI achieves ($R^2$=0.4445), outperforming DLPL-Cap (0.3622) by 23\%.  
On the small digital case, \timectrl, GAI reduces MAE by 13\% compared to the prior state-of-the-art (DLPL-Cap: 0.0701 vs. GAI: 0.0610).  
While BMC implementation of balanced MSE exhibits high absolute loss values (71.6–152.3) due to its batch-based normalization, it maintains competitive MAE/$R^2$ metrics (e.g., 0.065 MAE on \sarray~0.7407 on \timectrl). This suggests BMC effectively handles label imbalance but requires careful loss scaling.  
Prior methods consistently underperform, particularly on large-scale designs (\ultra, \sandwich). For example, ParaGraph’s $R^2$ drops to 0.32 on \ultra, while GAI retains ($R^2$=0.4358).  
These results validate CircuitGCL’s ability to learn transferable representations for parasitic prediction, particularly when paired with GAI’s analytical integration for imbalanced regression.  


