\subsection{Extended Study on Label Rebalancing}

To further demonstrate the effect of balanced loss functions, we conduct an extended study, where we directly use bmsCE as the loss function of prior baselines. As depicted in \autoref{fig:ext_bsm}, using bsmCE yields significant accuracy improvements across all baselines. CircuitGPS benefits most from bsmCE, suggesting its original loss function was poorly suited for imbalanced classification.
DLPL-Cap shows consistent but moderate gains (3.2--52.8\%), indicating its native loss already partially addresses label imbalance.
CircuitGCL maintains robust improvements (25.2--62.8\%), demonstrating bsmCE’s compatibility with its representation scattering mechanism.
Large-scale designs (\ultra, \sandwich) universally benefit, with improvements exceeding 45\% for all methods, highlighting bsmCE’s scalability.

\autoref{fig:mse_gain} demonstrates the performance improvements of Balanced MSE across data-scarce regions. The label distribution of \timectrl reveals highly imbalanced samples: dense data at mid-range targets (0.4–0.6, 20k samples) versus near-zero data at extremes (e.g., 0.8–1.0). MSE gain shows balanced MSE significantly enhances model performance in low-data regions: negative gains (poor baseline) shift to near-zero or slight positive gains (0.0–0.1) at 0.8–1.0, effectively mitigating bias from data scarcity. Medium/low-sample regions (0.6–0.8) also achieve gains of 0.3–0.4. The method improves generalization in data-scarce scenarios while maintaining stability in high-sample regions.

\section{Conclusion}
CircuitGCL’s self-supervised paradigm and distribution-aware losses address two universal EDA challenges: data scarcity (via GCL) and label imbalance (via rebalancing). Its graph-native architecture aligns naturally with circuit netlists, enabling seamless adoption in commercial tools for tasks requiring rapid design-space exploration. 
This positions CircuitGCL as a foundational framework for next-generation EDA tools, bridging the gap between data-driven automation and precision-critical circuit design.
