\subsection{Interpretability In Finance}

Financial AI models are integral to modern decision-making, driving risk management, fraud detection, credit assessments, and algorithmic trading and many other financial applications. However, their complexity raises concerns about transparency, compliance, and accountability. This section explores how interpretability techniques are applied in finance, aligning them with the key categories of explainability: feature attribution, model simplification, visual and concept-based methods, behavioral analysis, and mechanistic interpretability. By mapping explainability techniques to financial tasks, we illustrate how interpretability ensures regulatory compliance, enhances transparency, and mitigates financial risks.

\subsubsection*{Feature Attribution}

Feature attribution techniques assess the influence of individual financial attributes on model predictions, ensuring fairness and transparency. For example, models like SHAP \citep{Lundberg2017} and LIME \citep{Ribeiro2016} quantify the impact of financial variables such as income, debt levels, and credit history in determining loan approvals. Additionally, SHAP values are used in fraud detection models \citep{Zhang2020} to analyze the impact of transaction patterns on fraud risk. Gradient-based attribution techniques like Integrated Gradients \citep{Sundararajan2017} highlight critical risk factors affecting financial forecasts, though they do not explain deeper causal relationships.

\subsubsection*{Model Simplification}

Model simplification methods approximate complex AI models with interpretable surrogates, balancing accuracy and transparency. In regulatory compliance, decision trees and rule-based models \citep{Hayashi2016} offer structured rules for credit risk assessment, bankruptcy prediction, and fraud detection. Additionally, techniques like distillation \citep{Zhang2020} compress deep learning models into interpretable structures, making AI-driven financial systems more transparent while retaining predictive accuracy.

\subsubsection*{Visual Interpretability in Algorithmic Trading}

Visual interpretability helps investors and analysts understand AI-driven market predictions. For example, attention mechanisms \citep{Selvaraju2016} highlight key market indicators affecting stock movements.

\subsubsection*{Behavioral Interpretability For Market Stability And Fraud Detection}

Behavioral interpretability treats AI models as black boxes, examining how they react to input changes. Sensitivity and perturbation analysis \citep{Casalicchio2018} evaluates how AI-driven financial strategies perform under market shifts, while minimal pair analysis \citep{Warstadt2020} helps identify inconsistencies and biases in AI-driven fraud detection systems.

