\subsubsection{Auto-Interpretation}  
\label{sec:auto_interp}
SAE features, though often interpretable, lack direct human-readable interpretations. To convert these features into understandable insights, in order to get the human-readable labels as per \href{https://www.neuronpedia.org/llama3.1-8b/steer}{Neuronpedia \cite{Neuronpedia2024}
} - a project initiated by Google's DeepMind, features are assigned labels like "references to London" by using an AI model to analyze the patterns in the texts where a feature is most strongly activated. These patterns are derived from the "Top Activations" of the feature, allowing the larger AI to identify and label the feature's focus based on the text it interacts with.

The usefulness increases as human-readable labels make it easier to understand, interpret, and explain the feature's role in the model, enhancing its interpretability and application.

Furthermore, the \textbf{magnitude of feature activation} in sparse autoencoders helps identify essential features, and enables performing numerical calculations, aiding in feature selection and statistical analysis for better decision-making.

Section~\ref{sec:sae_feature_discovery} shows our experiments related to the use of identifying the credit risk and bias using auto-interpretation of sparse features.

\subsubsection{Self-Interpretation} \label{selfinterp}
Another approach to get the human-readable interpretation is self-interpretability. Self-interpretability is a novel but intuitive concept described in \href{https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/8ev6coxChSWcxCDy8/self-explaining-sae-features}{Self-explaining SAE features}  and originally conceived in \citep{chen2024selfieselfinterpretationlargelanguage}. The idea is to ask the model to describe a feature that is being steered via SAE autoencoder latents as defined $h$ in Equation \ref{eqn:relu}. 

Section~\ref{sec:sae_self_interpretability} demonstrates our use of self-interpretability to extract clusters of financial features from large language models.


\subsubsection{Steering Sparse Autoencoders}
\label{sec:steering_sparse_autoencoders}

In feature steering, SAEs' specific features or latents of interest are modified to artificially high or low values during the forward pass (see \href{https://transformer-circuits.pub/2024/scaling-monosemanticity/index.html\#appendix-methods-steering}{Methodological Details} for implementation details). Feature steering is remarkably effective at modifying model outputs in specific, interpretable ways. It can be used to modify the model’s demeanor, preferences, stated goals, and biases; to induce it to make specific errors.

Using the definition of SAE decoder reconstruction in equation \ref{eqn:w_dec}, we can modify sparse hidden state $h$ with a steering vector $\Delta{h}$.

\[
\hat{x}' = W_{\text{dec}}(h + \Delta{h})
\]

Sparse autoencoders (SAEs) described earlier have emerged as a powerful tool for interpretability. Instead of modifying raw activations, SAEs learn \textbf{disentangled representations} that allow targeted interventions in model behavior \citep{Bricken2023}.

Section~\ref{sec:feature steering} presents our research experiments demonstrating how feature steering enables modification of LLM's output related to credit risk and enhances performance in sentiment analysis.


\subsection{Mechanistic Interpretability Methods: Observation vs. Intervention}
\label{sec:mech_interp_types}

Mechanistic interpretability research is divided into two major categories as shown in Figure \ref{fig:Mechanistic Interpretability Methods}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Observation-Based Methods} – Analyze model activations without modifying the model.
    \item \textbf{Intervention-Based Methods} – Actively modify neural computations to establish causal relationships.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=.7\linewidth]{Categories_Obs_Int.png}
  \caption{Interpretability Methods}
  \label{fig:Mechanistic Interpretability Methods}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Observation-based Methods}

Observation-based methods in mechanistic interpretability analyze a large language model's (LLM) internal activations without modifying their parameters. Sparse autoencoder-based feature detection helps reveal hidden patterns, showing how models organize and process information across layers. These techniques identify emerging features and the mechanisms driving predictions, enhancing interpretability.

They can be broadly categorized into different frameworks: \textbf{Logit Lens}, \textbf{Sparse Autoencoders}, and Other Methods such as structured probing and feature visualization. Given that Sparse Autoencoders were introduced in Section~\ref{sec:sae_basic}, this section covers Logit Lens and other observation-based techniques in mechanistic interpretability.



\subsubsection{Logit Lens}
\label{sec:logit_lens}
One of the fundamental approaches to observing neural activations is the \textbf{Logit Lens} which applies the next-word prediction at every layer. This enables researchers to analyze how confident a model is at predicting outputs at different layers, offering a glimpse into the step-by-step evolution of predictions. Mathematically, for an intermediate activation $h_\ell$ at layer $\ell$, the Logit Lens maps it to vocabulary space using the final model output projection matrix $W_{\text{out}}$ and bias $b_{\text{out}}$ from the pre-trained model:

\[
\hat{y}_\ell = W_{\text{out}} h_\ell + b_{\text{out}}
\]

where $\hat{y}_\ell$ represents the logits at layer $\ell$. Here, $W_{\text{out}}$ and $b_{\text{out}}$ are directly inherited from the model's original language modeling head, trained to predict the next token at the final layer. For example, in GPT models, these parameters are optimized to map the last hidden state to vocabulary logits during training. If earlier layer logits closely resemble final outputs, it suggests that the model forms predictions incrementally rather than exclusively at the last layer.

Section~\ref{subsec:logit_lens} demonstrates our use of Logit Lens to analyze token prediction dynamics across layers in financial sentence prompts.



\subsubsection{Other Observational Techniques}

\textbf{Probing for Features:} Feature probing involves training external classifiers on intermediate activations to test whether a model encodes specific properties. Simple linear probes \citep{alain2016} examine linear separability, while structured probes attempt to identify high-level concepts.

\textbf{Feature Visualization:} Feature visualization techniques generate synthetic inputs that maximally activate neurons, revealing their sensitivity to different patterns. Activation Maximization \citep{zeiler2014} iteratively adjusts inputs to highlight neuron-preferred features.

\textbf{Attention Analysis:} Observing attention distributions can offer interpretability insights in transformer models. Attention Lens \citep{sakarvadia2023} maps attention head activations to vocabulary tokens to uncover hidden feature relationships.



\subsection{Intervention-Based Methods}

Interventional techniques provide a causal framework for understanding and modifying model behavior by perturbing activations or weights and measuring the resulting effects. 

Intervention techniques in mechanistic interpretability include methods such as activation patching, steering sparse autoencoders, and causal abstraction. Given that steering sparse autoencoders was already covered in Section~\ref{sec:steering_sparse_autoencoders}, we now turn to the remaining intervention techniques in the following section.



\subsubsection{Attribution Patching}
\label{sec:attribution_patching}
One of the most widely used interventional methods is attribution patching, which involves modifying input data to determine how specific features contribute to the model’s predictions. This technique helps to attribute the model’s decisions to particular components of the input, providing insights into which aspects of the data are most responsible for the model's outputs. Attribution patching can be linked to techniques such as feature attribution \citep{Sundararajan2017}, and Shapley value-based analysis \citep{Lundberg2017}.

Attribution patching involves the following key steps:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Running the model with a \textbf{clean input} to obtain the baseline predictions.
    \item Modifying or \textbf{corrupting} certain parts of the input, effectively "patching" or removing specific features.
    \item Running the model again on the modified input and observing the changes in the output.
    \item The \textbf{attribution of importance} is determined by measuring how much the modified input alters the predictions, thereby indicating the significance of each part of the input.
\end{enumerate}

As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:attribution_patching}, this process compares an original model (left) correctly outputting \textbf{buy}, when evaluating Tesla stock data against a modified version (right) with patched activations, producing \textbf{sell} with the central intervention point represented by the \textbf{Patch} node that modifies specific neural components. The methodology follows a structured four-step attribution patching approach: identifying relevant components (price/volume features, technical indicators), implementing targeted activation patches, measuring resulting signal differences, and establishing causal relationships between internal mechanisms and trading decisions. This rigorous approach extends beyond correlational analysis to determine which components are causally necessary for specific computational pathways, enabling researchers to trace information flow through trading algorithms and precisely identify the mechanisms responsible for particular buy or sell recommendations.

