\caption{Attribution patching analysis}
\label{fig:attribution_patching}
\end{figure}

Section~\ref{subsec:attr_patch_experiment} demonstrates our experiment on how attribution patching reveals influential heads and layers in financial sentence predictions using clean and corrupted prompts.

\subsubsection{Other Intervention Techniques}

In addition to attribution patching and SAE steering, several alternative intervention techniques exist:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Causal Abstraction}: Maps neural computations onto human-understandable structures, making high-level AI reasoning transparent \citep{xia2024neural}.
    \item \textbf{Resample Ablation}: Replaces activations with \textbf{r}andomized values to test the stability of circuits in transformers \citep{Zhang2023}.
    \item \textbf{Feature Injection}: Introduces synthetic activations to study how specific concepts influence outputs \citep{lin2024}.
\end{itemize}


\section{Applications in Financial Services}
As mentioned in Section~\ref{sec:mech_interp_types}, Mechanistic Interpretability techniques can be broadly classified into \textbf{observation-based methods} and \textbf{intervention-based methods}. Observation techniques passively analyze how a model processes information, whereas intervention techniques actively modify its activations to test causal effects.

Although these methods, such as sparse autoencoder-based feature analysis, activation steering, and attribution techniques, show promise in enhancing transparency, bias mitigation, and compliance, they are not yet widely known or implemented in real-world financial systems. They represent potential applications that could improve AI-driven decision-making in areas like credit scoring, fraud detection, and algorithmic trading.

Some potential use cases for applying mechanistic interpretability techniques in finance are mentioned below and their detailed applications are illustrated in Table \ref{tab:InterpFinance}.

\subsection{Observation-Based}

\subsubsection{Feature Discovery And Identification}

Observation-based techniques like \textbf{Logit Lens} mentioned in Section~\ref{sec:logit_lens} and \textbf{sparse autoencoders} mentioned in Section~\ref{sec:sae_basic} are great tools to identify the features of the model. The Logit Lens applies the next-word prediction every layer, showing how the model's guesses evolve and become more refined across layers. This helps understand model decision-making in areas such as credit scoring and fraud detection. Sparse autoencoders, on the other hand could recover monosemantic features from a small one-layer transformer. Together, these methods increase model transparency and risk mitigation. 

\subsubsection{Feature Activation-Based}

The magnitude of feature activation of SAE essentially provides a quantifiable measure of the model's focus on specific aspects of the input or output, enabling a deeper understanding of its decision-making process and facilitating diverse applications across various domains. By analyzing activation patterns, models can identify inconsistencies in generated content, detect biases in decision-making, and extract insights for trading strategies. In hallucination detection, feature activation helps flag potentially incorrect outputs. For bias detection, it highlights sensitive attributes that may influence model decisions, ensuring fairer outcomes. In trading strategies, feature activation aids in mapping financial news sentiment to market movements, enabling more informed predictions. This approach enhances transparency and reliability in AI-driven financial analysis.


\subsection{Intervention-Based}

\subsubsection{Attribution Patching}

As mentioned in Section~\ref{sec:attribution_patching}, attribution patching tests the impact of neuron activations by modifying specific layers in a model. A typical example is using corrupted and clean financial reports—where a model predicts bankruptcy for one company based on a report, and then a corrupted version of the same report is used to analyze which activations determine the final prediction. This method helps in identifying which components of a model contribute most significantly to financial distress predictions and fraud detection.


\subsubsection{Steering With SAEs}
Feature steering as described in Section~\ref{sec:steering_sparse_autoencoders} modifies model behavior internally, reducing reliance on prompt engineering and fine-tuning. In sentiment analysis, for example, it can direct models toward specific risk assessments or investment strategies. This enables precise AI-driven financial decisions without costly retraining. However, fine-tuning is necessary when introducing new knowledge since feature steering only adjusts existing activations rather than incorporating external information.



