\section{Experimental Results (Without Sparse Autoencoders)}
\label{sec:use_cases_without_sae}

The experiments presented in the following sections build directly on the foundational concepts discussed earlier, applying them in practical financial scenarios. For Logit Lens and Attribution Patching, we primarily use GPT-2 models to analyze token probability evolution and activation importance across layers. For Sparse Autoencoder-based experiments, Google’s Gemma models are primarily employed to uncover and steer interpretable features. Together, these experiments demonstrate how different interpretability techniques can be systematically applied to understand and enhance model behavior in financial applications.

\subsection{Logit Lens}
\label{subsec:logit_lens}

In this experiment, \textbf{Logit Lens} is employed to track how probabilities shift across layers, allowing for a clearer understanding of which parts of the model influence specific financial predictions. We used the \textbf{GPT-2} model for this analysis.

First, we used the Logit Lens to generate a heatmap as shown in Figure \ref{fig:Logit} illustrating the model's predicted token probabilities at each layer for a given financial prompt. The specific sentence used was:

\begin{quote} \textit{"With good earnings the stock price of company will likely"} \end{quote}

In the heatmap, the X-axis represents each input token from the prompt, and the Y-axis represents the model layers from 0 to 10. Each cell shows the model's most probable next word prediction given the preceding context at that particular layer, with color intensity indicating the associated probability. The probability reflects the model's confidence that the word it predicts is the correct next token given the previous tokens. This visualization allows us to observe how the model’s guesses evolve and become more refined as the input flows deeper into the network.


\begin{figure}[h]
