\section{Experimental Results Using Sparse Autoencoders}
\label{sec:sae_use_cases}
In this section, we explore Sparse Autoencoder-based applications, taking a deeper step into feature-level interpretability. We first discuss how to label features through Feature Discovery and Identification, followed by experiments on Prediction, Bias Reduction, and Hallucination Reduction. Most of the experiments are based on Sparse Autoencoders trained on Google's Gemma models, and the techniques demonstrated can be broadly extrapolated across different financial applications.

\subsection{Feature Discovery and Identification}
\label{sec:sae_feature_discovery}
Using sparse autoencoders for feature discovery can help identify and activate the most relevant features, improving interpretability and efficiency. By mapping neural behavior, sparse autoencoders can aid in targeted fine-tuning and support prompt engineering, structured knowledge representation, and improved decision-making.

\subsubsection{Self-Interpretability}
\label{sec:sae_self_interpretability}
As briefly described in \ref{selfinterp}, we are able to interpret and explain SAE latent representation of features via SAE steering. We simply prompt a model to describe a generic concept,  steer particular tokens via SAE latent and see a steered concept being described. An algorithm to generate a list describing all the features is the following: We steer (increase activation) one feature at a time across all the features available in our SAE, while providing the LLM with the following prompt: 

    \textit{What is the meaning of the word "X"?The meaning of the word "X" is}

The steering effect is focused on the word X in the prescribed prompt. The result is a complete list of SAE induced features described in plain English. Our experiments are using Gemma Scope
SAEs \citep{lieberum2024gemmascopeopensparse} trained on Gemma-2B \citep{gemmateam2024gemma2improvingopen} with dictionary size 16,384. A small sample of discovered finance related features is in Table \ref{tab:feature_description}.

