\subsection{Feature Steering}
\label{sec:feature steering}

As discussed in Section~\ref{sec:steering_sparse_autoencoders}, SAE’s feature steering is remarkably effective at modifying model outputs in specific, interpretable ways. It can be used to modify the model’s demeanor, preferences, stated goals, and biases; to induce it to make specific errors. Below we look at some examples.

\subsubsection{ Modifying Credit Risk Related Features}

In this experiment, we use feature steering to modify a model’s sentiment analysis of financial statements by shifting its focus toward credit risk. Instead of relying solely on prompt engineering, we steer specific features to adjust the model’s interpretation, demonstrating how steering can enhance context-aware financial analysis without requiring fine-tuning. This experiment was conducted using Neuronpedia, a DeepMind-supported platform referred to earlier.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{Steering1.png}
    \caption{Feature steering influence on sentiment analysis in financial statements}
    \label{fig:feature_steering_credit_risk}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig:feature_steering_credit_risk} demonstrates how feature steering influences sentiment interpretation in financial analysis. Initially, without steering, the model assigns a \textbf{mixed sentiment (3)} to the sentence: \textit{The company reported a significant drop in quarterly revenue but has successfully secured long-term financing and reduced outstanding debt}. The model primarily focuses on the negative impact of the \textit{revenue decline}, interpreting it as a sign of financial instability, while only partially considering the positive aspects of secured financing and debt reduction.

With feature steering applied at a high magnitude to the feature \textit{"concepts related to credit ratings and financial recovery"}, the sentiment shifts to \textbf{4}, highlighting both the \textit{negative (lower credit score)} and \textit{positive (secured financing \& debt reduction)} aspects, ultimately improving the overall outlook. This change occurs because steering shifts the model’s attention toward financially stabilizing factors, ensuring a more balanced and domain-specific interpretation.

This process might eliminate the need for extensive prompt engineering, as the model automatically emphasizes credit recovery factors without requiring manually reworded inputs like \textit{Analyze from a credit risk perspective}. Additionally, steering serves as a cost-effective alternative to fine-tuning, allowing existing models to dynamically adjust without requiring thousands of labeled financial statements.

Another key question is 'How much should we steer?' Feature steering alters model behavior in different evaluations and can introduce unintended trade-offs. Understanding whether its effects are predictable from feature activations or broader and harder to anticipate is crucial to refine its application (refer \textit{Toward Monosemanticity: Decomposing Language Models }~\cite{Anthropic2024}). Some studies have shown that excessive feature steering can lead to entangled representations.


\subsubsection{Credit Risk-Focused Steering on Sentiments}

A common financial application of AI models is extracting financial sentiment from a set of unstructured data, documents, or news sources. Basically, it is a classification problem of converting text input into a few classification categories.  

In our example, we have collected a set of manually-annotated sentences with the following 5 categories: very positive, somewhat positive, neutral, somewhat negative, and negative. These categories have been human labeled according to a severity of a news statement as it relates to the financial health of a company or the company's ability to sustain debt repayment. Using the Gemma-2B model with the following prompt: \emph{ Can you rate  the following sentence as a sentiment, very positive, somewhat positive, neutral, somewhat negative, very negative}, we are able to compare the unsteered and steered version of the sentiment classification.  The experiment makes an assumption that steering a particular feature related to Credit Risk will improve the quality of classification using LLMs.

The following confusion matrices (Figure~\ref{fig:cfm}) compare sentiment classification results before and after applying steering for feature 471, listed in Table~\ref{tab:feature_description}, which is identified as related to "credit risk". The left matrix represents classification results without steering, while the right matrix shows improvements after steering. 

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{ConfusionMatrix_3.png}
    \caption{Confusion matrices comparing sentiment classification performance before (left) and after (right) applying credit risk-focused steering}
    \label{fig:cfm}
\end{figure}

Without steering, the model frequently misclassified ``very positive'' and ``somewhat positive'' sentiments, with 769 instances of ``very positive'' misclassified as ``somewhat positive.'' Similarly, 132 neutral labels were mistakenly assigned to ``somewhat positive.'' Misclassification also occurred between ``somewhat negative'' and ``very negative,'' with 46 instances of ``very negative'' incorrectly predicted as ``somewhat negative.''

After applying credit risk-focused steering, classification accuracy improved across all sentiment categories. The correct classification of ``very positive'' increased from 654 to 953 (+299), while misclassifications into ``somewhat positive'' dropped by 296. Neutral misclassification errors declined from 132 to 36, reducing confusion with positive sentiments. The distinction between ``somewhat negative'' and ``very negative'' was also refined, reducing false negatives and improving overall credit risk sentiment differentiation.

Overall, steering significantly reduced false positives and negatives, aligning sentiment classification with credit risk considerations. The refined predictions demonstrate the effectiveness of domain-specific steering in financial sentiment analysis.



\section{Challenges}

Mechanistic interpretability in finance faces key challenges related to scalability, generalization, and feature-level transparency. While methods such as logit attribution, activation patching, and sparse autoencoder-based steering have improved AI explainability, scalability remains a major concern, especially as financial models grow to GPT-4 scale and beyond. Manual circuit analysis is infeasible for large models, and automated interpretability tools are still in early development. Furthermore, many existing techniques fail to generalize effectively to real-world financial applications, as models often rely on abstract representations that do not directly map to human-understandable financial concepts. The lack of ground-truth benchmarks for financial interpretability further complicates evaluating and validating AI-driven decision-making \citep{elhage2022b}.

Another critical challenge is the evolving nature of financial markets and regulations. AI models trained on historical data may struggle to adapt to changing risk environments, new financial instruments, and updated compliance laws. Dynamic interpretability pipelines that allow for real-time steering and adaptive model explanations are necessary to ensure AI models remain aligned with financial industry standards. The reliance on human evaluations in interpretability research also presents risks, as subjective analyses can introduce inconsistencies when explaining financial model decisions \citep{Rauker2023}.

To address these challenges, fully integrated interpretability tooling must combine techniques from various research efforts, such as Distill-style visualizations, Redwood’s tools, and Nanda’s TransformerLens \citep{nanda2022}. A unified framework could streamline analysis workflows, making it easier for financial professionals to debug models, trace decisions, and refine AI-driven predictions. Mechanistic interpretability is particularly valuable for financial institutions looking to improve credit risk modeling, algorithmic trading transparency, fraud detection, and regulatory compliance. By enhancing AI explainability in finance, institutions can build trust, improve governance, and ensure models remain robust in dynamic economic conditions \citep{hendrycks2023}.

