We are grateful to Jeffrey Nisen, Zirui Ding, Oksana Kityachik, Andrew Whiteley, and Navneet Mathur - our colleagues at Barclays for their comments, corrections and inspiration.

\section{Conclusion}

Mechanistic interpretability offers a promising route to dissect and guide AI-driven financial decisions with greater transparency and control. By combining observation-based methods (like logit attribution and sparse autoencoders) with intervention-based techniques (such as activation patching and feature steering), financial practitioners can pinpoint the neurons and attention heads most influential in tasks such as fraud detection, credit scoring, and compliance. Our experiments showed that these interpretability tools not only help isolate crucial risk or sentiment indicators but also allow dynamic model adjustments—cutting down on costly fine-tuning and enabling more precise responses in high-stakes scenarios.

In practice, mechanistic interpretability enhances AI reliability in finance. By replacing extensive prompt engineering and reducing reliance on fine-tuning, steering provides a cost-effective alternative for refining financial AI applications dynamically. These insights reinforce the need for continued research in the field of \textbf{financial AI safety}, particularly in the alignment of LLMs with regulatory standards and risk governance frameworks.

Despite its benefits, this approach faces notable challenges. Large-scale financial models can exceed current methods’ capacity to manually analyze circuits, and automated interpretability frameworks remain a work in progress. Financial features often blend abstract economic factors that are difficult to map to human-understandable concepts, and evolving markets or regulatory changes can outpace models’ training data. Additionally, identifying a particular neuron or attention head does not always clarify the exact influence of features such as credit score or volatility on the final decision—requiring integration with traditional model-agnostic methods for a truly comprehensive explanation.

\subsection{Future Work}

Looking ahead, the adoption of integrated interpretability pipelines will be crucial for ensuring AI-driven finance remains both transparent and adaptive. Combining mechanistic interpretability with feature-level attributions and real-time intervention tools can mitigate risks stemming from bias or market shifts. Future work can focus on integrating mechanistic interpretability with \textbf{feature-level attributions}, ensuring that AI decisions remain explainable not just at the neuron level but also in terms of real-world financial indicators. One promising direction is to integrate traditional statistical modeling techniques that utilize financial time series data with unstructured data processed by LLMs. This hybrid approach offers an explainable framework for combining structured and unstructured data, unlocking access to the vast potential of unstructured information.

As these methods mature, they will pave the way for robust, interpretable financial AI applications that can be safely aligned with regulatory standards and responsive to the dynamic demands of modern finance.




