\subsection{Simulations}\label{simi}

Our simulation protocols also follow from Ref. \cite{elizondo}. The reader is invited to visit Section 2.2 of this reference, which describes in all detail the technicalities involved. As explained there, our simulations are based on a MD custom code of our own creation, in which the cubic particles are modeled as regular hexahedrons of side length $d$, with uniform mass density $\rho_m=m/d^3$ and moment of inertia $I=md^2/6$ (Fig. \ref{simi}a). On the vertices of each hexahedron, eight spheres of radius $r_s=0.1d$ are placed, and bounded together with cylinders of the same radius and length $l_c=0.8d$ (Fig. \ref{simi}b), with the later used to represent the edges of the cubes. Inter-particle interactions were accounted for following the procedure outlined in \cite{elizondo}, which demonstrates that this simplified model system captures efficiently the structural and dynamical behavior of the present experimental system comprised by cubic particles. 

