Surprisingly, for all the relevant values of $\Gamma_f$ considered here, we observed approximately the same geometrical condition for the onset of melting, namely, that six cubes have escaped the monolayer, or $N_c=6$. We noticed that, when this condition is fulfilled, isolated nucleation domains of the \emph{liquid}-phase are formed and, later, these domains percolate to form a larger entity. Let us highlight that this condition corresponds to a relatively small lost in the dissipation regions of the lattice, which can be accounted for using the quantity $\delta\equiv 5d^2\cdot(N_c)/5d^2\cdot(900)$, that considers the ratio of the (ideal) dissipation region lost after $N_c$ cubes detach from the monolayer, to the (ideal) dissipation region of the initial configuration. Thus, for $N_c=6$, $\delta\approx0.007$. It may be of some value to highlight that this number is at least comparable to that corresponding to the hexagonal lattice of vibrated spheres reported in \cite{pacheco1}, in which the condition observed for an almost instantaneous sublimation is that a single sphere (with, ideally, seven point contacts) escapes from the monolayer, thus corresponding to a ratio of dissipation regions of $\delta\approx0.009$. 

