\textcolor{red}{\textbf{Gustavo, Francisco, Fernando:} During the last two decades there has been a very rich debate in the literature on the nature of the solid-liquid phase transition in 2D thermal systems. To the best of my knowledge, the most widely accepted description is the so-called Kosterlitz–Thouless–Halperin–Nelson–Young (KTHNY) theory, which explains how the unbinding of two types of topological defects destroys translational and orientational order, but at distinct temperatures. This leads to the possibility to obtain fluid states (defined in the conventional sense, i.e., in terms of canonical observables juts like $g(r)$, related to the translation degrees of freedom) which, however, still exhibit six-fold quasi-long-ranged orientational order. These states are commonly referred to as the \emph{tetratic phase}, for systems of hard squares, kytes, etc (see, for instance, Refs. \cite{mason,frenkel}, or https://doi.org/10.1039/D4SM01377H or https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-15723-w), or the hexatic phase, as observed in systems comprised by spherically symmetric repulsive particles (see, for example, J. Chem. Phys. 137, 104503 (2012), Phys. Rev. E 99, 022145 (2019), J. Chem. Phys. 141, 18C522 (2014) .... among many others!).} 

\textcolor{red}{I think it would be very relevant to determine and discuss (at least with simulations, but desirably, also with experiments) the $\Gamma_f$-dependence and $t$-evolution of orientational correlation functions of the form $g_n(r) \equiv \langle \psi_n(\mathbf{r})\psi_n(\mathbf{0}) \rangle$, with $\psi_n (\mathbf{r})=\frac{1}{N_B}\sum_{i=1}^{N_B} e^{i n \theta_j}$ ($n=4,6$), where $N_B$ is the number of nearest neighbors around an arbitrarily chosen particle at the position $\mathbf{r}$ , and with $\theta_j$ being the angle formed by the vector that localizes the jth-neighbor with respect to the selected particle, measured from an arbitrary reference axis. In terms of this correlation functions, $\displaystyle{\lim_{r\to\infty}}g_n(r)\neq 0$ for a crystalline state, $g_n(r)\sim r^{\delta}$ (power law decay) for the tetratic ($n=4$) / hexagonal ($n=6$) phase, or, $g_n(r)\sim e^{r/\xi_n}$ in the isotropic (liquid) phase, with $\xi_n$ being the orientational correlation length associated to the function $g_n$.}

\textcolor{red}{My guess in our present case is the following: Our system is not 2D, but quasi 2D, and some differences are expected intuitively . For instance, allowing the cubes to slightly move also in the $z$-direction is what leads to some sort of Brownian-like motion in the plane, where the cubes resemble to some extent the behavior of Brownian hard-disks in 2D. I would expect thus that, in spite of the geometry of the particles, the tetratic phase observed in hard-squares is suppressed,  but not necessarily the hexatic phase. If that would be the case, this would support our previous interpretation of the granular cubic system as an effective colloidal suspension in 2D, and it would be nice to observe the KTHNY scenario in this granular system! Lets give it a shot!}

