\dropcap{E}mergence occurs when a system displays new patterns, structures, or behaviors that cannot be easily understood by examining its parts in isolation \cite{britannica_emergence, oxford_emergence, oconnor1994, iep_emergence, sep_emergent, kivelson2016defining}. Also, depending on the question at hand, one can often discard a large amount of fine-grained information and still describe the system reliably. A widely used pedagogical example is bird flocking: no individual bird is aware of the overall formation, yet simple local rules—such as separation, alignment, and cohesion—produce collective behaviors like direction and density of the swarm. Other examples include synchronized firefly flashing and the decentralized organization of insect colonies \cite{matheny2019exotic}. 

From Aristotle’s holistic doctrine of \emph{form} and \emph{matter}—“the whole is something besides the parts” \cite{falcon2005aristotle}—the idea that wholes can display novel properties has passed through key milestones. John Stuart Mill distinguished additive \emph{mechanical} effects from qualitatively distinct \emph{chemical} ones \cite{mill1843logic}, and G.~H.~Lewes later coined “emergent” for the latter \cite{lewes1875}.  
In the early 20th century, British \textit{emergentists} proposed a layered ontology where higher-level laws supplement—and sometimes influence—lower-level processes \cite{alexander1920space,morgan1923emergent,broad1925mind}.  
Mid-century advances in quantum chemistry, molecular biology, and formal models of \textit{intertheoretic} reduction \cite{hempel1948studies,nagel1961structure,crick1958centraldogma} shifted the mainstream toward reductionism and sidelined emergentism.  
The debate resurfaced with arguments from multiple realizability, showing that high-level properties can arise from varied physical substrates \cite{putnam1975philosophy,fodor1974special,davidson1970mental}.  
Later contributions—from Anderson’s “more is different” paper \cite{anderson1972more} to Kim’s studies of supervenience and causal autonomy \cite{kim2006emergence}—have kept emergence central in the philosophy of science.

In recent years, the term has evolved from describing specific transitions, such as the “emergence of metabolism” \cite{bagley1990metabolism}, to becoming a popular buzzword, gaining more usage in scientific and non-scientific discourses  \cite{holland1998emergence, anderson1972more}. Fig.~\ref{fig:Ngram} shows this trend. In the following sections, we clarify why emergence can seem elusive, depending on the level of description used to explain a phenomenon.

