\section{Introduction}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The interest and establishment of the so-called \emph{fast-track procedures} for the registration of health products and medical devices is currently growing worldwide. Examples are conditional marketing authorisation of the EMA (see \cite{EMACondMarkAuth1} and \cite{EMACondMarkAuth2}), accelerated approval of the FDA (see \cite{FDAAccApp1} and \cite{us2024expedited}), listing processes for digital health applications and other accelerated approval pathways summarized in \cite{gupte2025regulatory}. 
The concept of fast-track procedures is as follows: The applicants have the opportunity to first apply for a conditional registration that provides a limited funding period and time to prepare the application for permanent registration. For conditional registration, typically products have to fulfil only a part of the requirements necessary for permanent registration and some kind of initial evidence. After conditional registration, applicants have a specific maximum period of time to meet all other requirements, including convincing evidence for permanent registration. The aim of fast-track procedures is that patients can benefit quickly from innovative products, particularly if a product fulfils an unmet medical need. Fast-track procedures also strengthen small companies because they can receive funding earlier. Because not all requirements may be fulfilled and fully convincing evidence may still be lacking after conditional registration, the potential risk and costs of a preregistered product must be lower than the benefit of its early availability. An example of a registration process for digital applications is the fast-track procedure for digital health applications (DiGA) in Germany. If a DiGA successfully completes the assessment of the BfArM, it is listed for reimbursement by statutory health insurance companies. Interest in this procedure is currently growing in more and more countries. Different variants of the German fast-track procedure of the BfArM for digital health applications are currently being established or have been established in other countries like France and Austria (see, for instance, \cite{overviewFastTrackEnglish} and \cite{overviewFastTrackGerman}). Our paper is mainly motivated by the German fast-track procedure for DiGA.  

%Different variants of the German fast-track procedure of the BfArM for digital health applications are currently being established or have been established in other countries like France and Belgium (see, for instance, \cite{overviewFastTrackEnglish} and \cite{overviewFastTrackGerman}). Our paper is mainly motivated by the German fast-track procedure for DiGA.  

In this paper, we consider different design options for fast-track registration processes, whereby we aim to account for the requirements of a conditional registration as well as the requirements for the permanent registration. This will include a careful discussion of the different requirements found in the guidelines, in particular, the BfArM guideline for the registration of DiGA in Germany \cite{leitfadendiga}. This is of interest to applicants, as \cite{ataiy2024digital} indicates.
Our focus is on overall valid and efficient designs that provide the possibility for a conditional registration and the flexibility with regard to the decision whether to use this possibility or directly strive for a permanent registration. We consider a design as overall valid if it provides overall type I error rate control, in the sense that the probability for a permanent registration of a DiGA without a positive health care effect is bounded by some level $\alpha$, e.g. for the one-sided level of $\alpha=0.025$. Our considerations will include the current standard of separate studies for conditional and permanent registration as well as two-stage adaptive designs (see, for instance, \cite{BuchWassmerBrannath2016}) that permit the use of the data of the conditional registration study also for the permanent registration. To the best of our knowledge, our paper is the first that systematically investigates the utility of adaptive designs in the context of a fast-track registration process. An important aspect of this assessment will be the overall performance of the whole two-step registration process, such as the overall success probability (power) and, as already mentioned, the type I error rate. In particular, we will identify situations where a sufficient overall success probability implies specific constraints for the overall study design. An example of such a constraint is the requirement of sufficiently large sample sizes in either the second or both steps, i.e., for the conditional and/or permanent registration. In addition, we will identify scenarios for which a two-stage registration process is less efficient than the direct application for an permanent registration without an intermediate conditional registration step. However, for some of these situations, a mixture of an adaptive two-stage and single-stage design provides an efficient alternative if we can decide with the first-stage data whether to follow the fast-track process or apply directly for permanent registration. Such options are possible with current DiGA registration regulations in Germany. 

Our article is arranged as follows. Section \ref{sectionExaplesFastTrack} gives a brief overview of the regulations of the established fast-track procedures. Section \ref{subsectionRequirements} provides a general discussion of requirements for conditional and permanent registration. Section \ref{NewChapterStudyDesignsFastTrack} discusses study designs under the assumption that an permanent registration is impossible or of no further interest after an unsuccessful conditional registration process. Reasons for this restriction could be a lack of resources without conditional registration or regulatory concerns with regard to an initially unsuccessful registration process. 
%\textcolor{red}{Subsection \ref{subsectionConsequences} investigates the impact of the discussed requirements on the overall power of the common design with two separate studies.} This is followed by a brief recap of two-stage adaptive designs and an analogue investigation of the overall power in \ref{subsectionAdaptiveDesigns}, and finally, subsection \ref{subsectionComparisonDesigns} compares the designs with regard to further performance parameters. 
In Section \ref{ChapterNonBindingFutility}, we will consider designs with a possibility of a data-dependent decision between the fast-track procedure and direct permanent registration, which guarantee overall type I error rate control. The paper concludes in Section~\ref{sec:Summary} with a detailed summary and discussion of our results as well as their implications.

\section{Examples of current Fast-Track Registration Processes}\label{sectionExaplesFastTrack}
We briefly outline the legal basis and regulatory requirements of the fast-track procedures for digital health applications established to date. The "app on prescription" in Germany has a pioneering role and was introduced by the Digital Healthcare Act (\emph{Ger.} Digitale-Versorgung-Gesetz, DVG), which came into force in 2019. Since then, digital health applications (\emph{Ger.} Digitale Gesundheitsanwendungen, DiGA) can be prescribed by physicians and psychotherapists and are reimbursed by statutory health insurance companies. The currently reimbursable DiGA are listed in the directory of reimbursable digital health applications (DiGA
directory \cite{digaDirectory}). To be listed in the DiGA directory (also called directory here), the applications must successfully complete the assessment of the Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices (\emph{Ger.} Bundesinstitut für Arzneimittel und Medizinprodukte, BfArM). The details of the registration process and the requirements for reimbursable DiGA are regulated by the Digital Health Applications Ordinance (\emph{Ger.} Digitale-Gesundheitsanwendungen-Verordnung, DiGAV). A concrete interpretation of that and more practical details can be found in the DiGA guideline \cite{leitfadendiga} of the BfArM. An old guideline version is also available in English; see \cite{oldLeitfadenEnglish}. 

There are two different possible ways of permanently being listed in the directory: following the fast-track procedure or applying for a permanent listing directly. The requirements to permanently be listed in the directory are the demonstration of at least one positive healthcare effect (via statistical significance) for a specific patient group as well as all quality and compliance standards from Sections 3 to 6 of the DiGAV. In order to prove a positive healthcare effect, a comparative study must be conducted and submitted to the BfArM. Furthermore, the study must be quantitative and fulfil known scientific standards. It must also be registered in a public trial registry. Compared to the application for a permanent listing, the requirements for a conditional listing are partly less strict because the study for the proof of a positive healthcare effect must not yet have been conducted or concluded. The quality and compliance standards from Sections 3 to 6 DiGAV must still be met. Instead of the proof of a positive healthcare effect via a significant study, the DiGA applicants must submit a so-called \emph{systematic evaluation of data}. The systematic evaluation of data must analyse data from the use of the DiGA which we call \emph{pilot data}. The pilot data must plausibly illustrate that a subsequent comparative study will be successful in proving a positive healthcare effect of the DiGA. According to the DiGA guide \cite[pp.~109-110]{leitfadendiga}, showing this initial evidence includes a relevant (numeric) positive healthcare effect that must be calculated from the pilot data. Further requirements for conditional listing are specified in the DiGA guide. The pilot data collection is intended to prepare the final study for the proof of the positive healthcare effect needed for permanent registration. For instance, this includes case numbers, measuring instruments, endpoints, and recruitment methods. The applicants have to submit the evaluation concept (including study protocol) for the final study when applying for conditional listing. Normally, the patient populations and endpoints investigated in the systematic evaluation of data and the final study should be the same. However, the selection of the primary endpoint for the final study can be done at the end of the pilot data collection. If an applicant receives conditional registration from the BfArM, the DiGA is listed in the directory for one year. Therefore, it is reimbursable by the statutory health insurance companies for at least one year. The proof of a positive healthcare effect via a comparative study must be submitted to the BfArM within the one year of conditional listing. Otherwise, the DiGA is removed from the directory, and the applicant can apply its DiGA for a further listing after one year at the earliest. In summary, the applicants have, with the fast-track procedure, one year more to conduct the comparative study to prove a positive healthcare effect while already being listed as a reimbursable DiGA. 

The "app of prescription" in Germany and the fast-track registration process for DiGA have inspired France to establish an analogue regulatory framework for so called \emph{Digital Medical Devices}, \emph{DMD} for short, (\emph{FR.} dispositifs médicaux numériques (DMN)) that are reimbursable by health insurance companies. In 2023, France adopted the fast-track registration process, which is called \emph{PECAN} (\emph{FR.} Processus de Certification des Applications Numériques de Santé) with Decree 2023-232 (see, for instance, \cite{overviewFastTrackFrance}). 

Many other countries are currently in the process of introducing or in discussion of implementing fast-track procedures for digital health applications, such as Belgium, Austria, Italy, and more (see, for instance, \cite{overviewFastTrackEnglish}, Table 1 in \cite{overviewFastTrackGerman}, \cite{AustriaDiGA} or \cite{ItalyDiGA}).


%The ‘app of prescription’ in Germany and the fast-track registration process for DiGAs have inspired France and Belgium to establish an analogue regulatory framework for digital health applications that are reimbursable by health insurance companies. We will briefly explain both. In 2023, France adopted the Fast-Track registration process, which is called \emph{PECAN} (\emph{FR.} Processus de Certification des Applications Numériques de Santé) with the Decree 2023-232 \textcolor{red}{Quelle}. Similar to the fast-track registration process in Germany, some initial clinical evidence must be shown for conditional registration of one year. The period of time to meet all requirements for permanent registration is six months and thus shorter. Belgium has created an authorisation pathway for digital health applications (\emph{FR.} Applications de santé mobiles) with the \emph{mHealth pyramid}, see \cite{mHealthBelgiumWebsite}, via the royal decree \cite{RoyalDecree2021}. For a conditional registration for three years (called \emph{Level 3 light} of the mHealth pyramid), it must be justified that the potential benefit of conditional reimbursement outweighs the risk of side effects for patients and the cost risk for the social insurance system (see guidelines which can be found on the INAMI website \cite{guidelineBelgium}). All requirements for permanent registration (\emph{Level 3 plus} of the mHealth pyramid), which includes clinical efficacy and a health economic added value, must be submitted no later than 30 months after conditional registration to the INAMI (National Institute for Health and Disability Insurance), which is responsible for the application process.

