As indicated in the lower branch of Figure~\ref{twoArms}, we use for $Z_1<z_f$ a constant second-stage sample size $I_{2,\text{const}}$, namely the one that provides us with the conditional success probability of $1-\beta$ given $Z_1<z_f$, for the a priori assumed effect $\delta=\xi\delrel$.  We are not aiming for a specific conditional power in this case, because this is costly in terms of sample size, particularly if $Z_1<z_f$, and there is no conditional registration that needs to be extended with a sufficiently high (conditional) probability. We are using the a priori assumed effect $\delta$, because a mid-trial effect re-estimation appears questionable without conditional power control. 

In order to also achieve an overall success probability  of at least $1-\beta$ at $\delta$ (i.e.\ over both branches in Figure~\ref{twoArms}), we need to have a conditional success probability of at least $1-\beta$ also in the upper branch, i.e.\ we need to require that 
\begin{align} \label{UpperBranchPower}
\mathbb{P}_{\delta}\left(Z_2\ge \Phi^{-1}\big(1-A(Z_1)\big)\middle|Z_1\geq\zf\right)\ge  1-\beta\,.
%\, \mathbb{P}_{\delta}\big(Z_1\ge \zf\big)\,.
\end{align}
Moreover, if $Z_1\geq \zf$ (and conditional registration is achieved), we choose, as in Section~\ref{NewChapterStudyDesignsFastTrack}, the second-stage sample size such that the conditional power $1-\beta$ is reached at the estimated effect estimate. As in Section~\ref{NewChapterStudyDesignsFastTrack}, condition \eqref{UpperBranchPower} can be satisfied with a sufficiently large minimum second stage information $\itwomin$. In contrast to the situation in Section~\ref{NewChapterStudyDesignsFastTrack}, we find now for all $\xi>  1$ such a minimal second stage information. This is because, for all $\delta>0$, the left-hand side of \eqref{UpperBranchPower} converges to 1 with increasing second-stage information.  

Lemma \ref{LemmaUniqueMinInformSecondStage} in Appendix \ref{AppendixProofs} shows for a design with conditional error function $A$ for all $\xi\geq 1$ and $I_1>0$, that there exists a unique minimal information $\itwomin$ such that (\ref{UpperBranchPower}) can be met with equality if $\Succ_A(I_1,0)\leq (1-\beta)\cdot\mathbb{P}_{\delta}(Z_1\geq\zf)$ (where $\Succ_A$ is as in \eqref{allgemeineConditionOverallPowerAdaptivD}, and if $\Succ_A(I_1,0)> (1-\beta)\cdot\mathbb{P}_{\delta}(Z_1\geq\zf)$ condition (\ref{UpperBranchPower}) is achieved with an arbitrary small information for the second stage. In this case, we can set $\itwomin=0$. 

We note that the use of the constant conditional error function, $A(z_1)=\alpha$ for all $z_1$, corresponds to the standard fast-track process if $Z_1\geq\zf$. For $Z_1<z_f$ it results in a new (second-stage) study for permanent registration with information $I_{2,\text{const}}=I_{\delta}$ and without using the pilot data. A potentially more efficient approach that utilizes the pilot data also for the permanent registration (in both cases) is to use a non-constant conditional error function like $A_{I,-\infty}$ or $A_{F,-\infty}$. Note that these conditional error functions should not include the binding futility stopping rule $Z_1<\zf$, because we are testing now $H_0$ also if $Z_1<\zf$. 

The proposed strategy of performing no adaptations at interim and so use a constant information $\itwoconst$ for the second stage whenever $Z_1<\zf$, particularly suggests to %consider the following test procedure: If $Z_1<\zf$ is observed, $H_0$ is tested by a
use a fixed-size sample z-test with %using 
the cumulative sample of both stages. That means, if $Z_1<\zf$ is observed, $H_0$ is rejected if 
\begin{align}
    Z=\sqrt{I_1/(I_1+\itwoconst)}Z_1+\sqrt{\itwoconst/(I_1+\itwoconst)}Z_2\geq \Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha)~.\label{fixedSizeSampleTest}
\end{align}
For our example fast-track procedure it holds $I_1=n_1/(2\sigma^2)$ and $\itwoconst=n_{2,\mathrm{const}}/(2\sigma^2)$ with sample sizes per group $n_1$ and $n_{2,\mathrm{const}}$. Thus, (\ref{fixedSizeSampleTest}) is equivalent to $$Z=\sqrt{n_1/(n_1+n_{2,\mathrm{const}})}Z_1+\sqrt{n_{2,\mathrm{const}}/(n_1+n_{2,\mathrm{const}})}Z_2\geq \Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha)~.$$

It can easily be seen (and is well known) that (\ref{fixedSizeSampleTest}) is equivalent to $Z_2\geq\Phi^{-1}(1-\tilde{A}_{Z,\alpha}(Z_1))$ for the specific inverse normal conditional error function 
$$\tilde{A}_{Z,\alpha}(z_1):=1-\Phi\left(\frac{\Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha)-\sqrt{I_1/(I_1+\itwoconst)}z_1}{\sqrt{\itwoconst/(I_1+\itwoconst)}}\right),\quad z_1\in\mathbb{R}$$ 
which satisfies the level condition (\ref{generalConditionCef}). To complete the design, for the upper branch, a conditional error function must also be set such that the type I error rate does not exceed $\alpha$. Here we choose $\tilde{A}_{Z,\alpha^{\prime}}$ where $\alpha\le \alpha^{\prime}\le 1$ is the largest possible value such that the Type I error rate of
%We truncate the conditional error function by $0.5$ for the same reasons as before. \textcolor{blue}{In order to obtain an overall increasing conditional error function, we also need to truncate the conditional error function of the fixed-size sample test. In summary}, we define
\begin{align}\label{definition_az}
    A_Z(z_1):= 0.5\wedge\begin{cases}
			\tilde{A}_{Z,\alpha^{\prime}}(z_1), & \text{if $z_1\geq \zf$}\\
            \tilde{A}_{Z,\alpha}(z_1), & \text{if $z_1<\zf$}~.
		 \end{cases}
\end{align}
is equal to or less than $\alpha$ (i.e.\ (\ref{generalConditionCef}) holds true).
%\textcolor{blue}{Note, that the truncation in the case $Z_1<z_f$ comes into play only if $\tilde{A}_{Z,\alpha}(z_f)> 0.5$, which implies $\tilde{A}_{Z,\alpha^{\prime}}(z_1)>0.5$ for all $z_1\ge z_f$. This, in turn, implies that for $Z_1\ge z_f$ we do not use the first stage data for the permanent registration and expect to receive permanent registration if $Z_2\geq \Phi^{-1}(0.5)=0$ or, equivalently, the second stage data provide a positive effect estimate. This appears to be a somewhat questionable design that would be difficult to justify. Hence, we will have to carefully investigate for which interim information fraction this will happen.} 
For all examples considered in the following, numerical calculations show that the significance level $\alpha$ is fully used, i.e. for $A_Z$ equality holds in (\ref{generalConditionCef}). Note that the proposed strategy is based on the Müller and Schäfer method \cite{muller2001adaptive} with the difference that the conditional error function $A_Z$ is truncated here by $0.5$.
%In the following, the truncation $\tilde{A}_{Z,\alpha}(z_1)\wedge 0.5$ for the lower branch will play no role because for the considered examples $\tilde{A}_{Z,\alpha}(\zf)<\alpha$ will always hold true. This implies that for the lower branch, a fixed-size sample test is always performed.
In line with the proposed strategy in Figure~\ref{twoArms}, we assume that $\itwomin$ and $\itwoconst$ are chosen such that the aimed conditional success probabilities specified in Figure~\ref{twoArms} are achieved.

In the following, we investigate and compare the performance of the conditional error functions $A_Z$, $A_{I,-\infty}$ and $A_{F,-\infty}$ and the non-adaptive design.

