\subsection{Investigation of the second stage information for the case $Z_1\ge z_f$}
Figure~\ref{fig:I2MinComparisonXiEquals1.25} compares the minimum second-stage information $t_{\xi}(I_{2,\min})$ (when $\xi=1.25$ and $\alpha_c=0.15$) for the case of a successful conditional registration ($Z_1\ge z_f$) between the designs. The minimum second-stage information is also represented in plots (a) to (d) of Figure~\ref{fig:BS} by the black solid lines.
%with the non-adaptive design ($A\equiv \alpha$) and the conditional error functions $A_{I,-\infty}$ and $A_{F,-\infty}$, respectively. 
The blue dashed lines in these plots show for the case $Z_1\ge z_f$
the maximum second-stage information when it exceeds $I_{2,\min}$. The maximum second-stage information is given by the adaptive second-stage information $I_2^{A}(z_f)$ (set $A\equiv\alpha$ for the non-adaptive design). These lines are linearly increasing in $t_\xi(I_1)$ as long as $\zf=\Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha_c)$, and they are constant or decreasing afterwards. 
%For Fisher’s product test and $A_Z$, the blue dashed line remains constant for $t_{\xi}(I_1)>1.13$ and $t_{\xi}(I_1)>0.94$, respectively, because the conditional error functions are then constantly equal to $0.5$.

With a small interim information, it can happen that the maximum information $I_2^{A}(\zf)$ becomes equal to the minimum information, in which case we need to use a constant second stage information also in the case $Z_1\ge z_f$, namely $I_{2,\min}$ (black solid line). For the non-adaptive design ($A\equiv \alpha$), this constant information equals $I_{\delta}$. Since $I_{2,\text{const}}=I_\delta$ also for $Z_1<z_f$,  there is no difference between the lower and upper case in Figure~\ref{twoArms}.
As a consequence, the pilot study is only done to "gamble" for a conditional registration. With $\xi=1.25$
and $\alpha_c=0.15$, this happens for all $t_{\xi}(I_1)\leq 0.137$. Such a "gambling" with rather small pilot data appears to be a quite questionable strategy. The gambling area of the non-adaptive design is indicated in plot (a) of Figure~\ref{fig:BS} by the grey horizontal dashed line.

In our example, a constant second-stage information after the conditional registration occurs for all $t_{\xi}(I_1)\leq 0.0813$ also with the inverse normal method, for all $t_{\xi}(I_1)\leq 0.0854$ with Fisher’s product test, and for all $t_{\xi}(I_1)\leq0.1128$ with $A_Z$,
i.e.\ for smaller pilot informations than in the non-adaptive case. For the adaptive designs the upper and lower cases can differ at least with regard to the constant second-stage information and the pilot data are always used in the application for permanent registration. Hence, the pilot data are never used only to "gamble" for a conditional registration.

\subsection{Overall comparison with regard to the second stage information}

Obviously, for the case $Z_1\ge z_f$, the data-driven second-stage information is always in the region between the solid black and dashed blue lines. One can see from Figure~\ref{fig:BS} that using a non-constant conditional error function requires much smaller second (and thereby overall) informations than the constant conditional error function $A\equiv\alpha$. With a sufficiently large pilot information, the region for the second-stage information can even be uniformly smaller for the adaptive designs than for the non-adaptive design. Moreover, also the constant information $\itwoconst$ for case $Z_1<z_f$
becomes smaller with the non-constant conditional error functions. This clearly indicates the efficiency gain by using an adaptive design with non-constant conditional error function in comparison to a non-adaptive design with $A\equiv\alpha$.

Figure~\ref{fig:BP03} allows for a comparison of the maximum information of the second stage between the designs. It can be seen that the non-adaptive design leads to a larger maximum information than the adaptive design with $A_Z$ if $t_{\xi}(I_1)\geq 0.14$, with Fisher’s product test if $t_{\xi}(I_1)\geq 0.17$ and with the inverse normal method if $t_{\xi}(I_1)\geq 0.2$. A comparison of the maxmimum information over both stages can be found in the Appendix (Figure~\ref{xi1.25MaxInformBothStages}).

Figure \ref{fig:BP04} shows for $\xi=1.25$ and $\alpha_c=0.15$ the average second stage information (relative to $I_\delta$) for the different designs when $\delta=\xi \delrel$.  The non-adaptive design has a larger second-stage information than the inverse normal method for $t_{\xi}(I_1)\geq 0.1398$ and Fisher’s product test  for $t_{\xi}(I_1)\geq 0.0742$. In particular, for the inverse normal method there is only a small non-gambling area for $t_\xi(I_1)$ where the non-adaptive design has a smaller mean information than the inverse normal method. For Fisher's product test and the adaptive design with $A_Z$ there is no such area. In particular, the adaptive design with $A_Z$ even has a lower mean information than the non-adaptive design for all considered $t_{\xi}(I_1)$. A comparison of the maxmimum information over both stages can be found in the Appendix (Figure~\ref{fig:BP07}).
