\paragraph{Notations of estimators}
The notations of estimators are clarified here. Given the sample size, an estimator can be viewed as a function that maps a sequence of observations to an estimated value, whether the observations are contaminated by noise or not. However, our main interest is to investigate its behavior when applied to both the noise-free sequence $\{X_{i}\}_{i=0}^{n}$ and the noisy sequence $\{Y_{i}\}_{i=0}^{n}$. Therefore, with a slight abuse of notations, we denote $[X, \sigma^2]_T$ as the estimate with the noise-free sequence, and $\widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}_T$ as the estimate with the noisy sequence. In contrast, $\langle X, \sigma^2\rangle_T$ is used to denote the true value of leverage effect, as defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:parameter-of-interest}. Specifically, the notations and definitions of the estimators in this paper are listed in Table~\ref{tab:notations-of-estimators}. In addition, the subscript $T$ in any estimator $[X, \sigma^{2}]_{T}$, $\widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}_T$ or parameter $\langle X, \sigma^{2}\rangle_{T}$ may be omitted when there is no ambiguity.

\paragraph{Expectations and variances}
By using $\E[h]$ for some function $h$, we mean that the expectation is taken with respect to the joint distribution of processes $(X_t)_{t\geq 0}$, $(\sigma_t)_{t\geq 0}$ and the noise $\{\eps_i\}_{i=1}^n$. We also consider the conditional expectation given processes $(X_t)_{t\geq 0}$ and $(\sigma_t)_{t\geq 0}$, which is denoted as $\E[h | \filt]$. The notations for variances and covariances should be interpreted similarly.

\paragraph{Overview of methodologies}
For an estimator of interest, we focus on two aspects:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Ensuring that it is \emph{unbiased with respect to noise}; that is,
\begin{equation}
\underbrace{\E\left(\left. \widehat{[X, \sigma^2]_T}  \right| \filt \right) - [ X, \sigma^2 ]_T}_{\text{bias due to noise}} = 0.
\end{equation}
\item Assessing its total variance by decomposing it into the \emph{variance due to discretization} and the expected \emph{variance due to noise}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:variance-decomposition}
\begin{aligned}
\var\left(\widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}_T \right)
&= \var\left[\E\left(\left. \widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}_T  \right| \filt \right)\right] +  \E\left[\var\left(\left. \widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}_T  \right| \filt \right)\right] \\
&= \underbrace{\vphantom{\left[\var\left(\left. \widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}_T \right| \filt \right)\right]}\var\left(\left[ X, \sigma^2 \right]_T\right)\vphantom{\left[\var\left(\left. \widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}_T \right| \filt \right)\right]}}_{\text{variance due to discretization}} + \E\Bigl[\,\underbrace{\var\left(\left. \widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}_T \right| \filt \right)}_{\text{variance due to noise}}\,\Bigr].
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
To establish the limit theorems, the idea of Equation~\eqref{eq:variance-decomposition} needs to be made more rigorous. Yet, a primary goal of this paper is to balance the asymptotic variances arising from these two sources by developing new estimators and tuning hyperparameters.

\section{Construction of estimators}\label{sec:construction}
\subsection{Original estimator}
We start with an existing continuous leverage effect estimator proposed by \cite{aitsahalia2017EstimationContinuousDiscontinuous}. This estimator uses the threshold technique to deal with the jump in the observed log-price process. However, since we are not including jumps in this paper, we will only consider its form without threshold. When the noise is absent, their estimator is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:yacine-noisefree}
    [ X, \sigma^{2} ]^{[\text{AFLWY17}]}_{T} = \frac{1}{k_n \Delta_n}\sum_{i=k_{n}}^{n - k_{n} - 1} (\Delta X_{i}) \left(
        \sum_{j=i+1}^{i+k_n} (\Delta X_{j})^2 - \sum_{j=i-k_n}^{i-1} (\Delta X_{j})^2
    \right).
\end{equation}
Notice that $(k_{n}\Delta_{n})^{-1}\sum_{j=i+1}^{i+k_{n}} (\Delta X_{j})^{2}$ and $(k_{n}\Delta_{n})^{-1}\sum_{j=i-k_{n}}^{i-1} (\Delta X_{j})^{2}$ are spot volatility estimates for $\sigma_{i+1}^2$ and $\sigma_i^2$ respectively, where $k_n\to \infty$ and $k_n \Delta_n \to 0$ as $n\to \infty$. By directly substituting $Y_{i}$ for $X_{i}$ in this estimator, the estimator becomes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:yacine-noisy}
    \widehat{[ X, \sigma^{2} ]}^{[\text{AFLWY17}]}_{T} = \frac{1}{k_n \Delta_n}\sum_{i=k_{n}}^{n - k_{n} - 1} (\Delta Y_{i}) \left(
        \sum_{j=i+1}^{i+k_n} (\Delta Y_{j})^2 - \sum_{j=i-k_n}^{i-1} (\Delta Y_{j})^2
    \right).
\end{equation}
Given the processes, the conditional expectation of the noisy estimator is
\begin{equation}
\E\left(\left.\widehat{[ X, \sigma^{2} ]}^{[\text{AFLWY17}]}_{T}\right| \filt \right)
=
[ X, \sigma^{2} ]^{[\text{AFLWY17}]}_{T} + 2\frac{n-2k_n}{k_n \Delta_n}\nu_3.
\end{equation}
Therefore, the dominant term is the noise term whenever $\nu_3$ is non-zero. This is because, for the $i$th summand in the estimator, the noise terms at $i$ and $i+1$ exist simultaneously in the estimates of $\Delta X_i$ and $\Delta \sigma_i^2$. In fact, the estimator is inconsistent even when $\nu_3$ is zero, as the variance due to noise diverges as $n \to \infty$.

\subsection{All-observation estimator}\label{sec:all-observation}
Therefore, our first attempt is to shift the windows for spot volatility estimates outward by $\Delta_{n}$, which does not affect the asymptotic behavior of the noise-free version of estimator. The resulting estimator is called an \emph{all-observation Leverage Effect} (LE) estimator, and its noise-free version is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:all-observation-noisefree}
[X, \sigma^{2}]^{\rm (all)}_T
=
\frac{1}{k_{n}\Delta_{n}}
\sum_{i\in I} (\Delta  X_{i}) \left(
\sum_{j\in I^{+}(i)} (\Delta X_{j})^{2} - \sum_{j\in I^{-}(i)}(\Delta X_{j})^{2}
\right),
\end{equation}
where $I^{+}(i) = \{i+2, \dotsc, i+k_{n}+1\}$, $I^{-}(i) = \{i-k_{n}-1, \dotsc, i-2\}$, and $I = \{k_{n}+1, \dotsc, n-k_{n}-2\}$. It is clear that $|I^{+}(i)| = |I^{-}(i)| = k_{n}$ and $|I| = n - 2k_{n} - 2$. The noisy version of this estimator is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:all-observation-noisy}
\widehat{[X, \sigma^{2}]}^{\rm (all)}_T = \sum_{i\in I} (\Delta Y_{i}) \left(\widehat{\sigma}_{(i+1)+}^{2} - \widehat{\sigma}_{i-}^{2}\right),
\end{equation}
where the spot volatility estimates are
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\sigma}_{(i+1)+}^{2} = \frac{1}{k_{n}\Delta_{n}} \sum_{j\in I^{+}(i)} (\Delta  Y_{j})^{2},
\quad
\widehat{\sigma}_{i-}^{2} = \frac{1}{k_{n}\Delta_{n}} \sum_{j\in I^{-}(i)}(\Delta 
 Y_{j})^{2}.
\end{equation}

The bias and variance due to noise are described by the following proposition. The proof, as well as a more precise version, is given in Appendix~\ref{app:all-observation-noise}.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:all-observation-noise}
If $\{\eps_{i}\}_{i=0}^{n}$ satisfies the condition (i) and (ii) concerning the noise in Section~\ref{sec:model-settings-and-notations}, given the processes, the all-observation estimator is unbiased, \emph{i.e.}
\begin{equation}
\E \left( \left. \widehat{[X, \sigma^{2}]}^{\rm (all)}_T \right| \filt \right) = [X, \sigma^{2}]^{\rm (all)}_T.
\end{equation}
and its conditional variance satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{eq:all-observation-noise-variance}
\Delta_n^3 k_n^2
\var\left(\left. \widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}^{\rm (all)}_T \right| \filt \right)
\convp
(8 \nu_2 \nu_4 + 16 \nu_2^3 + 8 \nu_3^2) T, \quad \text{as } n\to\infty.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

Although the all-observation estimator is unbiased due to noise, its variance is of order $O(n^3 k_n^{-2})$, and therefore diverges. According to the asymptotic results derived in \cite{aitsahalia2017EstimationContinuousDiscontinuous}, the variance due to discretization is of order $O(k_n^{-1} + n^{-1} k_n)$. Therefore, it is never possible to achieve a balance between the discretization variance and the noise variance by simply choosing an appropriate $k_n$.

However, this estimator is the foundation for the subsequent sections. We will show that the variance due to noise can be controlled using subsampling, averaging, and multi-scale techniques, allowing us to strike a balance with the variance due to discretization.



\subsection{Subsample estimator}\label{sec:subsample}
We now consider using a sparse subsample of the observations. Recall the notations defined in Section~\ref{sec:model-settings-and-notations}. Hereafter, we assume that $k_n\to\infty$, $H_n\to\infty$ and $k_nH_n\Delta_n \to 0$ as $n\to\infty$.
The noise-free version of the estimator based on the subsample $(H_n, h)$ is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:subsample-noisefree}
    [ X, \sigma^{2} ]^{(H_n, h)}_T = 
    \frac{1}{k_{n}H_{n}\Delta_{n}}
    \sum_{i\in I_{H_n,h}} (\Delta_{H_n} X_{i_{h}}) \left(
    \sum_{j\in I^{+}(i)} (\Delta_{H_n} X_{j_{h}})^{2} - \sum_{j\in I^{-}(i)}(\Delta_{H_n} X_{j_{h}})^{2}
    \right),
\end{equation}
where $I^{+}(i) = \{i+2, \dotsc, i+k_{n}+1\}$, $I^{-}(i) = \{i-k_{n}-1, \dotsc, i-2\}$, and $I_{H_n,h} = \{k_{n}+1, \dotsc, n_{H_n,h}-k_{n}-2\}$. Clearly, $|I^{+}(i)| = |I^{-}(i)| = k_{n}$, and $|I| = n_{H_n,h} - 2k_{n} - 2$.
The noisy version of this estimator is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:subsample-noisy}
    \widehat{[ X, \sigma^{2} ]}^{(H_n, h)}_T = \sum_{i\in I_{H_n,h}} (\Delta_{H_n} Y_{i_{h}}) \left(\widehat{\sigma}_{(i+1)_{H_n,h}^+}^{2} - \widehat{\sigma}_{i_{H_n,h}^-}^{2}\right),
\end{equation}
where the spot volatility estimates are
\begin{equation}
    \widehat{\sigma}_{(i+1)_{H_n,h}^+}^{2} = \frac{1}{k_{n}H_{n}\Delta_{n}} \sum_{j\in I^{+}(i)} (\Delta_{H_n} Y_{j_{h}})^{2},
    \quad
    \widehat{\sigma}_{i_{H_n,h}^-}^{2} = \frac{1}{k_{n}H_{n}\Delta_{n}} \sum_{j\in I^{-}(i)}(\Delta_{H_n} Y_{j_{h}})^{2}.
\end{equation}

The variance of the subsample estimator can be directly derived from the properties of all-observation estimator by substituting $n$ with $n_{H_n,h}\approx n/H$. Thus, the variance due to discretization is of order $O(k_n^{-1} + n^{-1} H_n k_n)$, while the variance due to noise is of order $O(n^3 H_n^{-3} k_n^{-2})$. Although the variance due to noise is much smaller than that of the all-observation estimator, it still diverges because of the assumption that $k_n H_n \Delta_n \to \infty$ as $n\to \infty$.

\subsection{SALE: subsampling-and-averaging estimator}\label{sec:SALE}
Since we have $H_n$ subsamples, we can average the subsample estimators to reduce the variance due to noise. The \emph{Subsampling-and-Averaging Leverage Effect} (SALE) estimator for a noise-free sequence is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:SALE-noisefree}
[ X, \sigma^{2} ]^{(H_n)}_T = \frac{1}{H_n} \sum_{h=1}^{H_n} [ X, \sigma^{2} ]^{(H_n, h)}_T,
\end{equation}
and the noisy version is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:SALE-noisy}
\widehat{[ X, \sigma^{2} ]}^{(H_n)}_T = \frac{1}{H_n} \sum_{h=1}^{H_n} \widehat{[ X, \sigma^{2} ]}^{(H_n, h)}_T.
\end{equation}

The SALE is unbiased due to noise, as implied by Proposition~\ref{prop:all-observation-noise}. It also allows the noise to be strictly stationary and dependent up to a certain lag. Specifically, when the noise is independent, there is no covariance due to noise between subsample estimators. Consequently, the variance of SALE due to noise can be significantly reduced compared to the subsample estimator. The following proposition describes the variance of SALE estimator due to noise. The proof, along with a more precise version, is given in Appendix~\ref{app:SALE-noise}.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:SALE-noise}
If $\{\eps_{i}\}_{i=0}^{n}$ satisfies the condition (i) and (ii') concerning the noise in Section~\ref{sec:model-settings-and-notations}, denoting the generalized autocorrelation functions (acfs) of noise as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:general-acf}
\begin{aligned}
\rho_2(l) &= \corr(\eps_i, \eps_{i+l})
= \frac{\E [ \eps_{i}\eps_{i+l} ]}{\nu_2}, \\
\rho_3(l) &= \corr(\eps_i, \eps_{i+l}^2)
= \frac{\E [ \eps_{i}\eps_{i+l}^{2} ]}{\sqrt{\nu_2(\nu_4 - \nu_2^2)}}, \\
\rho_4(l) &= \corr( \eps_{i}^{2}, \eps_{i+l}^{2} )
= \frac{\E[ \eps_{i}^{2} \eps_{i+l}^{2} ] - \nu_2^2}{\nu_4 - \nu_2^2},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
for $l \in \Z$, assuming $2q < H_{n}$, we have
\begin{equation}
\Delta_n^3 H_n^4 k_n^2
\var \left(\left. \widehat{[ X, \sigma^{2} ]}^{(H_n)}_T \right| \filt \right)
\convp
\Phi T,
\quad \text{as } n\to\infty,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Phi}
\Phi = 8 \nu_2 \left(\nu_4 - \nu_2^2\right)
\sum_{l=-q}^{q} \Bigl( \rho_2(l)\rho_4(l) + \rho_3(l) \rho_3(-l) \Bigr)
+ 24 \nu_2^3 \sum_{l=-q}^{q} \rho_2^3(l).
\end{equation}
Specifically, if $\{\eps_{i}\}_{i=0}^{n}$ is independent, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Phi-independent}
\Phi = 8 \nu_2 \nu_4 + 16 \nu_2^3 + 8 \nu_3^2.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

Although the variance due to noise can be significantly reduced to $O(n^3 H_n^{-4}k_n^{-2})$ by subsampling and averaging, the variance due to discretization does not improve significantly. As Theorem~\ref{thm:SALE-clt-noisefree} will demonstrate, the variance due to discretization is $O(k_n^{-1} + n^{-1} H_n k_n)$, the same as the subsample estimator. Suppose $H_n \asymp n^{a}$ and $k_n \asymp (n/H)^b$. Then, the minimum order of total variance is $O(n^{-1/7})$ when $a = 5/7$ and $b = 1/2$, resulting in an optimal convergence rate of $n^{-1/14}$. Although the variance of SALE estimator converges, the convergence rate is not satisfactory.

The SALE estimator can also be viewed as being composed of overlapping sparse increments. That is,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:SALE-increments}
\widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}_T^{(H_n)}
=
\frac{1}{H_n} \sum_{i=(k_n+1)H_n}^{n-(k_n+2)H_n} (\Delta_{H_n} Y_i)
\Bigl(
\widehat{\sigma}_{H_n, i+}^2 - \widehat{\sigma}_{H_n, i-}^2
\Bigr),
\end{equation}
where the spot volatility estimations are
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\sigma}_{H_n, i+}^2 = \frac{1}{k_n H_n \Delta_n}
\sum_{j=i+2H_n}^{i+(k_n+1)H_n}(\Delta_{H_n} Y_j)^2, \quad
\widehat{\sigma}_{H_n, i-}^2 = \frac{1}{k_n H_n \Delta_n}
\sum_{j=i-(k_n+1)H_n}^{i-2H_n}(\Delta_{H_n} Y_j)^2.
\end{equation}

\subsection{MSLE: multi-scale estimator}\label{sec:MSLE}
Finally, we consider combining SALE estimators at different scales to achieve improved performance. The \emph{Multi-Scale Leverage Effect} (MSLE) estimator is defined as a weighted average of SALE estimators at different scales. In general, consider a set of scales $1 \leq H_1 < \dots < H_{M_n} \leq n^a$ for some $a\in(0,1)$ and $M_n>0$. The noise-free version of the MSLE estimator is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:MSLE-noisefree}
[X, \sigma^2]^{\rm (MS)}_T = \sum_{p=1}^{M_n} w_{p} [X, \sigma^2]^{(H_p)}_T,
\end{equation}
and the noisy version is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:MSLE-noisy}
\widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}^{\rm (MS)}_T = \sum_{p=1}^{M_n} w_{p} \widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}^{(H_p)}_T,
\end{equation}
where the weight vector $\bm w=(w_1, \dotsc, w_{M_n})$ satisfies $\bm w^T \bm 1_{M_n} = 1$ and $\|\bm w\|_\infty$ bounded.

It is clear that the MSLE is unbiased due to noise, and its variance due to noise is determined by the covariance due to noise between SALE estimators at different scales. However, the behavior of covariance due to noise can be very different depending on the assumption on noise. If the noise is independent, the covariance due to noise between SALE estimators at different scale is simply zero. The only exception occurs when one scale is exactly twice the other, in which case the covariance is non-zero but small (for normal distributed noise, the correlation coefficient equals to 0.1). For dependent noise, the covariance due to noise can be non-zero, especially for scales that are close to each other, but it is tiny between most of the scales. The following proposition describes the variance of MSLE due to noise when the noise is independent, and its proof is given in Appendix~\ref{app:MSLE-noise}. For dependent noise, we do not derive a closed-form expression for the variance due to noise, because the covariances due to noise is influenced by too many factors, but it can be calculated using the algorithm described in Appendix~\ref{app:MSLE-noise}, which has been validated through simulation.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:MSLE-noise}
Suppose $\{\eps_{i}\}_{i=0}^{n}$ satisfies conditions (i) and (ii) concerning the noise in Section~\ref{sec:model-settings-and-notations}. Moreover, suppose $k_p = \lfloor \beta \lfloor n/H_p\rfloor^b \rfloor$ holds for all $p\in\{1, \dotsc, M_n\}$, with some constant $\beta>0$ and $b\in(0,1)$, and define the matrix $\bm F\in \R^{M_n\times M_n}$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:MSLE-noise-Fpq}
F_{p,q} = (8\nu_2\nu_4 + 16\nu_2^3 + 8\nu_3^2) 1_{\{p=q\}} + 2\nu_2(\nu_4-\nu_2^2) (1_{\{H_p/H_q=2\}} + 1_{\{H_q/H_p=2\}}).
\end{equation}
Then, for any $p, q \in \{1, \dotsc, M_n\}$, we have
\begin{equation}
\Delta_n^3 H_p^2 H_q^2 k_p k_q \cov \left(\left. \widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}^{(H_p)}_T, \widehat{[X, \sigma^2]}^{(H_q)}_T \right| \filt \right)
\convp
F_{p,q} T.
\end{equation}
Therefore, the variance due to noise of MSLE is given by
\begin{equation}
\frac
{\var \left(\left. \widehat{[ X, \sigma^{2} ]}^{\rm (MS)}_T \right| \filt \right)}
{\displaystyle \Delta_n^{-3} \sum_{p=1}^{M_n} \sum_{q=1}^{M_n}
\frac{w_p}{H_p^2 k_p} \cdot F_{p,q} \cdot \frac{w_q}{H_q^2 k_q}}
\convp
T.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
Although under the iid noise setting, SALE estimators at different scales can be correlated due to noise when the ratio of scales is $2$ or $1/2$, such correlation is weak. For example, it is easy to see that $\bm F$ is a strictly diagonally dominant matrix satisfying $\sum_{q\neq p} |F_{p,q}| < \frac{1}{2} |F_{p,p}|$. As a result, the variance due to noise in MSLE estimator is dominated by the diagonal elements of $\bm F$, so its order is equivalent to that of the ``independent case'' where $\bm F$ is diagonal. Figure~\ref{fig:base-estimators} compares our all-observation estimator with the continuous leverage effect estimator in \cite{aitsahalia2017EstimationContinuousDiscontinuous}, and also illustrate a shifted version of all-observation estimator. This shifted all-observation estimator can be used to construct SALE estimators that are completely uncorrelated with each other due to noise and have smaller asymptotic variances due to noise. A similar proposition can be established, with the corresponding version of Equation~\eqref{eq:MSLE-noise-Fpq} being $F_{p,q}^{(\text{shifted})} = (8\nu_2\nu_4 + 8\nu_2^3 + 8\nu_3^2) 1_{\{p=q\}}$.
\end{remark}

