\section{Simulation study}\label{sec:simulation}

The asymptotic properties of the proposed SALE and MSLE estimators are validated for three circumstances: the noise-free situation, the iid noise situation, and the dependent noise situation. Both infeasible and feasible CLTs are considered. Furthermore, the finite sample performance of the MSLE estimator is compared with other methods: the all-observation estimator in the noise-free setting, and the pre-averaging estimator in the noisy setting.

\subsection{Validation of the asymptotic properties}\label{sec:simulation-clt}
\subsubsection{Data generating processes}
The Heston model \citep{heston1993ClosedFormSolutionOptions} is used to generate the discrete values of the underlying continuous processes. The model is defined as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\ud X_t &= \left(\mu - \frac{\sigma_t^2}{2}\right) \ud t + \sigma_t \ud W_t, \\
\ud \sigma_t^2 &= \kappa (\theta - \sigma_t^2) \ud t + \gamma \sigma_t \left(\rho \ud W_t + \sqrt{1-\rho^2} \ud B_t \right).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Here, $X_{t}$ represents the log-price process, and $V_{t} = \sigma_{t}^{2}$ represents the volatility process. The model is driven by independent Brownian motions $W_{t}$ and $B_{t}$. The leverage effect in this model is $\langle X, V \rangle_{T} = \gamma \rho \int_{0}^{T} V_{t} \ud t$. The parameters are set as follows: $\mu=0.02, \kappa=5, \theta=0.04, \gamma=0.5, \rho=-0.7$. The initial values are set as $X_0 = 0, V_0 = 0.02$. Furthermore, we set $T=1/252$ and $n=23400$, and simulate 5000 paths for each case.

In the \emph{noise-free} situation, the discrete values of log-price process $\{X_i\}_{i=0}^n$ can be directly observed. In the noisy situations, the observations are contaminated by noise $\{\eps_i\}_{i=0}^n$ with zero mean and a variance of $\varsigma^2$.

For the \emph{iid noise} situation, we consider the following distributions for $\{\eps_i\}_{i=0}^n$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Normal: $\eps_i \sim \normal(0, \varsigma^2)$;
\item Uniform: $\eps_i \sim \unif(-\sqrt{3}\varsigma, \sqrt{3}\varsigma)$;
\item Skew normal: the pdf of $\eps_i$ is $f(x) = 2\omega^{-1}\phi_\normal(\omega^{-1}(x-\xi)) \Phi_\normal(\alpha \omega^{-1} (x-\xi))$, where $\phi_\normal$ and $\Phi_\normal$ are pdf and cdf of the standard normal distribution, respectively. The parameters are $\xi=-\omega\delta\sqrt{2/\pi}$, $\omega=\varsigma (1-2\delta^2/\pi)^{-1/2}$, where $\delta=\alpha (1+\alpha^2)^{-1/2}$. The shape parameter $\alpha$ is set as 1.
\end{enumerate}

For the \emph{dependent noise} situation, both MA(2) and AR(1) processes driven by Gaussian white noise are considered. Note that MA(2) process satisfies our assumption of the dependent noise with $q=2$, whereas AR(1) process does not satisfy this assumption with any non-negative integer $q$. However, given the difficulty in determining the dependence level accurately for real-world noisy data, the AR(1) noise can be used to assess the robustness of the proposed estimators under a truncated dependence level $q$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item For the MA(2) process, we set
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ma2}
\eps_{i} = e_{i} + \theta_{1}e_{i-1} + \theta_{2}e_{i-2}, \quad
e_i \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \normal(0, \varsigma^2(1+\theta_1^2+\theta_2^2)^{-1}).
\end{equation}
The second and fourth moments of the process are
\begin{equation}
\nu_2 = \varsigma^2, \quad
\nu_4 = 2\varsigma^4(1+2\theta_1^2+2\theta_2^2+\theta_1^4+\theta_2^4+2\theta_1^2\theta_2^2),
\end{equation}
and the generalized acfs defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:general-acf} are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\rho_2(l) &= 1_{\{l=0\}} + \frac{\theta_{1}+\theta_{1}\theta_{2}}{1+\theta_{1}^{2}+\theta_{2}^{2}}1_{\{|l|=1\}} + \frac{\theta_{2}}{1+\theta_{1}^{2}+\theta_{2}^{2}}1_{\{|l|=2\}}, \\
\rho_3(l) &= 0, \\
\rho_4(l) &= 1_{\{l=0\}} + \frac{\theta_{1}^{2}\theta_{2}^{2}+\theta_{1}^{2}+2\theta_{1}^{2}\theta_{2}}{1+2\theta_1^2+2\theta_2^2+\theta_1^4+\theta_2^4+2\theta_1^2\theta_2^2} 1_{\{|l|=1\}}\\
&\qquad \qquad
+ \frac{\theta_2^2}{1+2\theta_1^2+2\theta_2^2+\theta_1^4+\theta_2^4+2\theta_1^2\theta_2^2} 1_{\{|l|=2\}}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The parameters $(\theta_1, \theta_2)$ are set to $(0.7, 0.5)$ and $(-0.7, 0.5)$.
\item For the AR(1) process, we set
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ar1}
\eps_i = \phi \eps_{i-1} + e_i, \quad e_i \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \normal(0, \varsigma^2\sqrt{1-\phi^2}),
\end{equation}
where $\phi \in (-1, 1)$ denotes the autoregressive coefficient. This is a common setting for modeling the stationary component of dependent noise \citep[see][]{jacod2017StatisticalPropertiesMicrostructure, li2022ReMeDIMicrostructureNoise}. The moments of the process are
\begin{equation}
\nu_2 = \varsigma^2, \quad
\nu_4 = 3\varsigma^4,
\end{equation}
and the generalized acfs are
\begin{equation}
\rho_2(l) = \phi^{|l|}, \quad
\rho_3(l) = 0, \quad
\rho_4(l) = \phi^{2|l|}.
\end{equation}
The parameter is set to $\phi = 0.7$.
\end{enumerate}

