\subsection{Experimental Setups}
All experiments in this paper are conducted on a single GPU, specifically the Nvidia RTX 3080, using PyTorch, while the extraction of common relation items is performed using MATLAB. The cross-subject setting for the entire experiment is illustrated in \hyperref[fig:Cross-subject setting]{Fig.~\ref*{fig:Cross-subject setting}}.

For the upcoming experiments, this paper adopts the Leave-One-Subject-Out (LOSO) validation strategy. This method is widely regarded as the most rigorous approach for cross-subject classification, as it completely excludes certain subjects during training and evaluates the model's performance on unseen subjects\cite{EEGNet}\cite{114}\cite{44}\cite{54}. It provides an objective measure of generalization ability. In contrast, other commonly used strategies, such as mix-up-all-subjects and random selection, either merge data from all subjects or partition the data randomly, which may artificially inflate classification performance and fail to reflect real-world adaptability. Given that an ideal BCI system should decode EEG signals from entirely unseen subjects, leave-n-subjects-out remains the most appropriate choice for evaluating cross-subject generalization\cite{64}\cite{91}\cite{wang2024depth}.

