\section{Plateaued Boolean functions and quantum distinguishing}

In~\cite{MRS19}, it was shown that the concept of plateaued-ness can be characterized via the derivatives, as well as the fourth moments.  

Recall that $f\in\mathcal{B}_{n}$ is called {\em plateaued of level $s$} if
$|{W}_f(\var{u})| \in \{0,2^{(n+s)/2}\}$, for all $\var{u}\in \F_2^{n}$, for a fixed integer $s$ depending on $f$ (we sometimes call $f$ an $s$-{\em plateaued} function).
If $s=1$ ($n$ must then be odd), or $s=2$ ($n$ must then be even), we call $f$ {\em semibent}. It was shown (we simplify that result for our purpose) in~\cite{MRS19} that 
for a Boolean function $f$ on $\F_2^n$, then $f$ is  $s$-plateaued   if and only if $\displaystyle \sum_{\var{a},\var{b}\in\F_2^n} (-1)^{D_\var{b} D_\var{a} f(\var{x})} =2^{n+s}$ (for all $\var{x}$) if and only if
$\displaystyle
\sum_{\var{d}   \in \F_2^n}  | W_f(\var{d})|^4=2^{3n+s}.
$

\section {The $\{I,H,N\}$--transforms}
\textcolor{red}{\bf Constanza may be better at this, as she and Matthew invented the stuff}

We consider the action of  $H,\,N,\,I$, respectively, on $\chi_f=\zeta^f$, $\zeta=e^{2\pi/q}$, for $f:\F_2\rightarrow {\mathbb Z}_q$ (here, we take $q=2^k$, for some integer~$k\geq 1$). If $k=1$, then we let
$$H\chi_f=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
1&1\\
1&-1
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{c}
\zeta^{f(0)}\\
\zeta^{f(1)}
\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
\zeta^{f(0)}+\zeta^{f(1)}\\
\zeta^{f(0)}-\zeta^{f(1)}
\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
H\chi_f(0)\\
H\chi_f(1)
\end{array}\right),$$
so, $H\chi_f(u)=\sum_{x\in\F_2}\zeta^{f(x)}(-1)^{u x}$. Next,
$$N\chi_f=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
1&i\\
1&-i
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{c}
\zeta^{f(0)}\\
\zeta^{f(1)}
\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
\zeta^{f(0)}+i\zeta^{f(1)}\\
\zeta^{f(0)}-i\zeta^{f(1)}
\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
N\chi_f(0)\\
N\chi_f(1)
\end{array}\right),$$
so, $N\chi_f(u)=\sum_{x\in\F_2}\zeta^{f(x)}(-1)^{ux}i^x$.
Finally, $I\chi_f(u)=\zeta^{f(u)}$.

Suppose now that we defined the action of $U'=\Pi_{j\in R_N'}N_j\Pi_{j\in R_H'}H_j$, where $R_N',\,R_H'\subseteq \{1,\ldots,n-1\}$ over $f:\F_2^n\rightarrow \F_2$. Due to the property $(A\otimes B)\cdot(C\otimes D)=AC\otimes BD$, we can separately describe the action of $H_j,\,N_j$ and $I_j$, as the action of $U$ will be the matrix multiplication of these.

Let $U\in\{I,H,N\}^{\otimes n}$ be defined as $U=\Pi_{j\in R_N}N_j\Pi_{j\in R_H}H_j$, where, for any $2\times2$ matrix $A$, $A_j=I\otimes I\otimes \cdots\otimes \stackrel{j}{A}\otimes I\otimes \cdots\otimes I$, and let $R_I=\{1,\ldots,n\}\setminus(R_N\cup R_H)$. Let $\var{c}=(c_1,\ldots,c_n)\in\F_2^n$ be defined by $c_j=1\Leftrightarrow j\in R_N$. The spectrum of a generalized Boolean function $f$ is then given by
$$U\chi_f(\var{u})=\sum_{\var{x}\in\F_2^n,x_j=u_j\ \forall j\in R_I}\zeta^{f(\var{x})}(-1)^{\sum_{j\in R_N\cup R_H}u_jx_j+s_2^\var{c}(\var{x})}i^{\sum_{j\in R_N}x_j},$$
where $s_2^{\var{c}}(\var{x})=\sum_{j<k}(c_jx_j)(c_kx_k)$. 

A function is called $U$-bent if $|U\chi_f(\var{u})|=2^{\frac{n}{2}}\ \forall \var{u}$, and $s$-$U$-plateaued iff $|U\chi_f(\var{u})|\in\{0,2^{\frac{n+s}{2}}\},\ \forall \var{u}$. 

