\begin{document}

\title{Weinstein neighbourhood theorems for stratified subspaces}

\author{Yael Karshon}
\author{Sara Tukachinsky}
\author{Yoav Zimhony}

\subjclass[2020]{53D05, 58A35 (Primary); 53D12, 58A40  (Secondary)}

\begin{abstract}
By analogy with Weinstein's neighbourhood theorem, we prove a uniqueness result for symplectic neighbourhoods of a large family of stratified subspaces.
This result generalizes existing constructions, e.g., in the search for exotic Lagrangians.
Along the way, we prove a strong version of Moser's trick and a (non-symplectic) tubular neighbourhood theorem for these stratified subspaces.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle
\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Normal form theorems}\label{subsection:normal_form_theorems}

Weinstein's Lagrangian neighbourhood theorem~\cite{WEINSTEIN1971329} states that a neighbourhood of a Lagrangian submanifold $L$ in a symplectic manifold is symplectomorphic to a neighbourhood of the zero section in the cotangent bundle $T^*L$. More generally, Weinstein~\cite[Lecture~5]{weinstein_lectures} used the tubular neighbourhood theorem and Moser's trick \cite{mosers_trick} to prove the following symplectic normal form theorem. Let $\left(M_0, \w_0\right)$ and $\left(M_1, \w_1\right)$ be symplectic manifolds, $N_0,N_1$ submanifolds, and $g:N_0 \to N_1$ a diffeomorphism. Then there exists a symplectomorphism extending $g$ if and only if there exists a symplectic bundle isomorphism
\begin{equation*}
    G:\left(TM_0\Big\lvert_{N_0}, \w_0\right) \to \left(TM_1\Big\lvert_{N_1}, \w_1\right)
\end{equation*}
over $g$ which restricts to $Dg$ on $TN_0$. If we futher assume that $N_i$ are coisotropic, then the existence of such $G$ is equivalent to $g^*\w_1 = \w_0$ as forms on $N_0$~\cite{coisotropic_imbedding}.

These symplectic normal form theorems provide local models around submanifolds, which enable explicit constructions and calculations. Such models are used in the study of Hamiltonian group actions, in the search for exotic Lagrangians (``Lagrangian knots'' \cite{first_steps_in_symplectic_top,polterovich2024lagrangian}), and more.

There are versions of such normal form theorems where the submanifolds $N_i$ are replaced with singular subspaces. For example, there are symplectic normal form theorems around the following singular subspaces:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item\label{example:nodel_fiber} A Lagrangian 2-sphere with one positive self-intersection,
    used in \cite{MR1852770} to construct generalized symplectic rational blowdowns.
    \item\label{item:2_mutation_conf} A \emph{Mutation configuration}, which is a Lagrangian $2$-torus with an attached Lagrangian disc
    \cite[Lemma~4.11]{PASCALEFF2020106850}, used in~\cite[Section~4]{PASCALEFF2020106850} to analyze wall-crossings of potential functions of monotone Lagrangian $2$-tori.
    \item \emph{Solid mutation configurations}, which are generalizations of \cref{item:2_mutation_conf} to higher dimensions, used in~\cite{solid_mutation_conf} to construct infinitely many exotic montone Lagrangian tori in projective spaces.
    \item\label{example:good_lagrangian_pinwheels} \emph{Good Lagrangian pinwheels}~\cite[Lemma~3.4]{symplectic_rational_blowup}, used in~\cite{brendel2024semi} to construct exotic tori in non-monotone $4$-manifolds.
    \item\label{example:Ac_buildings} Ac-buildings \cite[Subsection~3.3]{alvarezgavela2022arborealization}, which are certain singular subspaces with \emph{arboreal singularities}.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Results}\label{subsection:introduction_results}

In this paper, we prove normal form theorems around certain singular subspaces: stratified subspaces that are \emph{smoothly locally trivial with conical fibers}, introduced in \cite{zimhony2024commutative}. This condition controls the type of allowed singularities and can be checked locally around points. For a stratified subspace
\begin{equation*}
    A = \bigcup_{X\in \S}X \subset M,
\end{equation*}
it reads that for every stratum $X\in \S$ and every $p\in X$, a neighbourhood $U$ of $p$ in $M$ is diffeomorphic to $\R^k \times \R^{n-k}$ such that
\begin{itemize}
    \item $X\cap U$ is identified with $\R^k\times\{0\}$;
    \item For every $Y\in \S$ with $\overline{Y}\supset X$ a nearby stratum, $Y\cap U$ is identified with $\R^k\times C_Y$ for a subset $C_Y\subset \R^{n-k}\setminus \{0\}$ invariant under multiplication by $t\in(0,1)$.
\end{itemize}
We assume this condition for all stratified spaces throughout the introduction.
Throughout the paper, we endow these subspaces with the induced subspace differential structure in the sense of Sikorski. See \Cref{section:stratified_differential_spaces} for formal definitions.

This family of singular subspaces includes all the examples given at the end of \Cref{subsection:normal_form_theorems}, as discussed in \Cref{subsubsection:existing_normal_forms}. Another example is (not necessarily regular) zero level sets of momentum maps \cite[Theorem~7.9]{zimhony2024commutative}.

Our main results are \Cref{thm:main_coisotropic,thm:main_symplectic}. \Cref{thm:main_coisotropic} is a symplectic normal form theorem around \emph{coisotropic} stratified subspaces, i.e., stratified subspaces whose Zariski tangent spaces (\Cref{definition:Zariski_tangent}) are coisotropic.

\begin{mainTheorem}\label{thm:main_coisotropic}
    For $i=0,1$, let $\left(M_i, \w_i\right)$ be symplectic manifolds and $\left(A_i, \S_i\right)$ stratified subspaces of them, smoothly locally trivial with conical fibers. Assume that $A_i$ are coisotropic and that there exists a stratified diffeomorphism $g:\left(A_0, \S_0\right) \to \left(A_1, \S_1\right)$ with $g^*\w_1 = \w_0$ as Zariski forms on $A_0$.
    Then there exist neighbourhoods $U_i\subset M_i$ of $A_i$ for $i=0,1$ and a symplectomorphism
    \begin{equation*}
        \G:(U_0, \w_0) \to (U_1, \w_1)
    \end{equation*}
    with $\G\big\lvert_{A_0}$ = $g$.
\end{mainTheorem}
Zariski forms are defined in \Cref{definition:zariski_forms}. For coisotropic submanifolds, \Cref{thm:main_coisotropic} recovers the classical result~\cite{coisotropic_imbedding}.

For stratified subspaces $A_0$ and $A_1$ that are not necessarily coisotropic, we require the additional data of a \emph{symplectic bundle isomorphism} ${TM_0\Big\lvert_{A_0} \to TM_1\Big\lvert_{A_1}}$, i.e., a diffeomorphism that is linear and symplectic on fibers.

\begin{mainTheorem}\label{thm:main_symplectic}
    For $i=0,1$, let $\left(M_i, \w_i\right)$ be symplectic manifolds and $\left(A_i, \S_i\right)$ stratified subspaces of them, smoothly locally trivial with conical fibers.

    Assume we have the following data:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item\label{assumption:existence_of_g} A stratified diffeomorphism $g:\left(A_0, \S_0\right) \to \left(A_1, \S_1\right)$.
        \item\label{assumption:existence_of_symplectic_G} A symplectic bundle isomorphism
        \begin{equation*}
            G:\left(TM_0\Big\lvert_{A_0}, \w_0\right) \to \left(TM_1\Big\lvert_{A_1}, \w_1\right)
        \end{equation*}
        over $g$ such that
        \begin{equation*}
            G\big\lvert_{T^ZA_0} = D^Zg,
        \end{equation*}
        where $T^ZA\subset TM\Big\lvert_A$ is the Zariski tangent and ${D^Zg:T^ZA_0 \to T^ZA_1}$ is the differential of $g$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then there exist neighbourhoods $U_i\subset M_i$ of $A_i$ for $i=0,1$ and a symplectomorphism
    \begin{equation*}
        \G:(U_0, \w_0) \to (U_1, \w_1)
    \end{equation*}
    with $\G\big\lvert_{A_0}$ = $g$.
\end{mainTheorem}

Note that \cref{assumption:existence_of_symplectic_G} implies in particular that $g^*\w_1 = \w_0$ as Zariski forms on $A_0$, which is explicit in \Cref{thm:main_coisotropic}. When the stratified subspaces are submanifolds, \Cref{thm:main_symplectic} recovers the classical result~\cite[Lecture~5]{weinstein_lectures}.

The proof relies on the following (non-symplectic) analogue of the tubular neighbourhood theorem for stratified subspaces.

\begin{mainTheorem}\label{thm:main_diffeomorphism}
    For $i=0,1$, let $M_i$ be manifolds and $\left(A_i, \S_i\right)$ stratified subspaces of them, smoothly locally trivial with conical fibers.

    Assume we have the following data:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item A stratified diffeomorphism $g:\left(A_0, \S_0\right) \to \left(A_1, \S_1\right)$.
        \item A vector bundle isomorphism
        \begin{equation*}
            G:TM_0\Big\lvert_{A_0} \to TM_1\Big\lvert_{A_1}
        \end{equation*}
        over $g$ such that
        \begin{equation*}
            G\big\lvert_{T^ZA_0} = D^Zg.
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate}
    Then there exist neighbourhoods $U_i\subset M_i$ of $A_i$ for $i=0,1$ and a diffeomorphism
    \begin{equation*}
        \G:U_0 \to U_1
    \end{equation*}
    which satisfies
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\G\big\lvert_{A_0}$ = $g$ and
        \item $D\G$ agress with $G$ along $A_0$, i.e., on $TM_0\Big\lvert_{A_0}$.
    \end{itemize}
\end{mainTheorem}

There is an interesting difference between the results of \Cref{thm:main_symplectic,thm:main_diffeomorphism}. In \Cref{thm:main_diffeomorphism} we obtain $D\G\big\lvert_{A_0} = G$, whereas in \Cref{thm:main_symplectic} this is
not guaranteed. The loss of this property happens because our version of Moser's trick, \Cref{thm:moser_trick}, uses a smooth weak deformation retraction instead of a strong one. In this weak version, the differential of the resulting symplectomorphism might fail to restrict to the identity along the stratified subspace. See \Cref{example:weak_moser_trick_differential} for this failure and \Cref{appendix:strong_deformation_retraction_moser} for a proof that in the strong case such failure cannot occur.

\Cref{thm:moser_trick} is Moser's trick for an arbitrary subspace $A$, provided that $A$ admits a smooth weak deformation retraction from a neighbourhood. Moreover, given a bigger subset $B\supset A$ which is invariant under the deformation retraction, we obtain a map which fixes $B$.

\begin{mainTheorem}\label{thm:moser_trick}
    Let $V$ be a manifold and $A\subset B\subset V$ closed subsets. Assume that $V$ admits a smooth weak deformation retraction $R:[0,1]\times V \to V$ to $A$ such that $B$ is an invariant subset of $R$. Let $\w_0, \w_1$ be two symplectic forms on $V$ that agree on $TM\Big\lvert_A$ and on $T^ZB$.
    Then there exist neighbourhoods $U',U'' \subset V$ of $A$ and a diffeomorphism
    \begin{equation*}
        \G:U'\to U''
    \end{equation*}
    such that
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\G^*(\w_1) = \w_0$ and
        \item $\G\big\lvert_{B\cap U'} = \Id$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{mainTheorem}

We refer to the neighbourhoods constructed in  \Cref{thm:main_coisotropic,thm:main_symplectic} as Weinstein neighbourhoods, by analogy with the case of Lagrangian submanifolds. We do not claim that they admit the structure of a Weinstein, or even Liouville, domain in general.
In the special case of \emph{isotropic} stratified subspaces, i.e., stratified subspaces whose strata are all isotropic, we prove in \Cref{thm:exactness_of_symplectic_form} that in a neighbourhood of the stratified subspace the symplectic form is exact --- by analogy to the case where the subspaces are isotropic submanifolds. This is the first step in addressing \Cref{question:Liouville_domain} below.

\Cref{lemma:lagrangian_stratified_equivalence} shows that a stratified subspace $A$ being isotropic and coisotropic, i.e., Lagrangian, is equivalent to the union of Lagrangian strata being dense in $A$. Combining \Cref{thm:main_coisotropic,thm:exactness_of_symplectic_form}, we see that a Lagrangian stratified subspace has a neighbourhood determined by the intrinsic geometry of the space and the restriction of the symplectic form to the Zariski tangent over its singular part. Moreover, the symplectic form restricted to this neighbourhood is exact. This is analogous to the case where the subspaces are Lagrangian submanifolds --- Weinstein's Lagrangian neighbourhood theorem \cite{WEINSTEIN1971329}.

\subsection{Applications}

\subsubsection{Existing normal form theorems}\label{subsubsection:existing_normal_forms}

All the singular subspaces described at the end of \Cref{subsection:normal_form_theorems} admit Lagrangian stratifications that are smoothly locally trivial with conical fibers. For example, a Lagrangian pinwheel can be decomposed into its core circle and an open 2-disc, and the local description given in \cite[Equation~(3.5)]{symplectic_rational_blowup} for \emph{good} Lagrangian pinwheels implies that their stratification is smoothly locally trivial with conical fibers. Similarly, a mutation configuration, which is a union of a Lagrangian 2-torus and a Lagrangian 2-disc, can be decomposed into the intersection and to the two complements, and the requirement that the intersection is clean implies that this stratification is smoothly locally trivial with conical fibers.

Thus, to obtain a normal form for these examples via \Cref{thm:main_coisotropic}, all that is needed is to construct a stratified diffeomorphism $g:A_0\to A_1$ and deform it around the singular part, so that $g^*\w_1 = \w_0$ as Zariski forms on $A_0$. Note that the Lagrangian assumption implies that such a deformation would only be necessary around the singular part, as both forms vanish on the regular part. We expect this construction can be done for each of the examples \cref{example:nodel_fiber}-\cref{example:good_lagrangian_pinwheels}. In example \cref{example:Ac_buildings}, there may be additional obstructions coming from the intrinsic geometry of the spaces and orientations.

\subsubsection{New normal form theorems and applications}
Brendel, Hauber, and Schmitz~\cite{brendel2024semi} define certain model spaces $B_{dpq}$, and use them to construct exotic tori for symplectic manifolds $M$ which admit a symplectic embedding $B_{dpq} \hookrightarrow M$. The Lagrangian skeleton of $B_{dpq}$, denoted by $L_{dpq}$, is a chain starting with a Lagrangian pinwheel and followed by $d-1$ Lagrangian spheres, such that every member of the chain intersects its neighbours transversally in one point. The singular part of $L_{dpq}$ is a disjoint union of the core circle of the pinwheel and $d-1$ Lagrangian transversal intersections. Since there are symplectic normal forms near every connected component of this singular part, and there are no obstructions coming from orientations, our results imply that if $L_{dpq}$ embeds in $M$ as a Lagrangian stratified subspace, then $B_{dpq}$ embeds in $M$ symplectically. Therefore, \cite[Theorem~B]{brendel2024semi} holds under the (a priori weaker) assumption $L_{dpq} \subset M$.

Pascaleff and Tonkonog \cite[Section~5]{PASCALEFF2020106850} describe a model space whose Lagrangian skeleton is a Lagrangian $n$-torus with an attached Lagrangian cone over an $(n-1)$-torus.
Our results provide a symplectic normal form around such singular subspaces.
The condition $g^*\w_1 = \w_0$ implies that the symplectic invariant described in \cite[Remark~5.7]{PASCALEFF2020106850} is preserved by $g$.
We believe one can use our symplectic normal form to define mutations of higher dimensional Lagrangian tori in more general cases. Compare with \cite[Section~5]{PASCALEFF2020106850}, which deals with the case where $M$ is toric, and \cite{solid_mutation_conf}, where the singular subspace is different.

Almost toric fibrations of symplectic $4$-manifolds~\cite{almost_toric} provide families of singular Lagrangian subspaces, often referred to as ``\emph{visible Lagrangians}'' \cite[Chapter~5]{Evans_book_2023}, and model neighbourhoods of them. Examples include
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Lagrangian $2$-tori (regular fibers);
    \item Lagrangian 2-spheres with one positive self-intersection (nodal fibers);
    \item Lagrangian pinwheels (``hitting an edge'' \cite[Section~5.3]{Evans_book_2023});
    \item mutation configurations and Lagrangian pinwheels with attached spheres can be described as unions of ``visible Lagrangians''.
\end{enumerate}

Given a singular Lagrangian configuration in a general symplectic manifold that is intrinsically diffeomorphic to a ``visible Lagrangian'' $L$ in some almost toric symplectic manifold, we can use the normal form theorem to identify its neighbourhood with a model neighbourhood of $L$. Within the model neighbourhood, ``almost toric manipulations'' such as node sliding can be performed, then pulled back to the original manifold. This generalizes the construction in \cite{brendel2024semi}, where $L=L_{dpq}$, and \cite[Section~4]{PASCALEFF2020106850}, where $L$ is a mutation configuration.

In the cases where $M$ is a del Pezzo surface or toric, Pascaleff and Tonkonog \cite[Theorems~1.2 and 5.7]{PASCALEFF2020106850} relate the disc potentials of a monotone Lagrangian torus $L_1$ and its mutation $L_2$. To this end they use \cite[Theorem~1.1]{PASCALEFF2020106850}, which reduces the question of matching their disc potentials in $M$ to the question of a non-vanishing Lagrangian Floer cohomology within a Liouville domain $U\subset M$ containing both Lagrangians, such that $L_1,L_2\subset U$ are exact. In \Cref{question:Liouville_domain} we ask whether our construction, around Lagrangian stratified subspaces, admits a structure of a Liouville domain. In \Cref{question:Lagrangian_skeleton} we ask if furthermore, such structure can have the Lagrangian stratified subspace as its Lagrangian skeleton. Positive answers to these questions would render \cite[Theorem~1.1]{PASCALEFF2020106850} applicable to the new mutations described above.

\subsection{Methods}

A key idea used in the proof of \Cref{thm:main_diffeomorphism,thm:main_coisotropic} is that gluing diffeomorphisms is hard, but gluing vector fields is easy. Euler-like vector fields along submanifolds (introduced by Bursztyn, Lima, and Meinrenken \cite{bursztyn2019splitting}) are defined using a local condition, can be glued together, and induce diffeomorphisms to the normal bundles. Moreover, some properties of thusly obtained diffeomorphisms can be read from properties of the associated Euler-like vector fields.

Hence, our strategy for proving \Cref{thm:main_diffeomorphism,thm:main_coisotropic} is to combine the Whitney Extension Theorem~\cite{whitney_extension} with Euler-like vector fields. The extension theorem allows us to \emph{locally} extend the given maps to locally defined diffeomorphisms of neighbourhoods. We use these local extensions to construct local Euler-like vector fields along strata which are related through the given maps, in a sense formally defined in \Cref{section:extension_thm_proof}. Finally, we use the properties of Euler-like vector fields to patch the vector fields together and obtain tubular neighbourhood embeddings. These give rise to diffeomorphisms of neighbourhoods.

To get that the differentials of the maps constructed in \Cref{thm:main_diffeomorphism} restrict to $G$ on $TM_0\Big\lvert_{A_0}$, we need to control the differentials of the tubular neighbourhood embeddings coming from Euler-like vector fields. To this end, we introduce the notion of \emph{concrete tubular neighbourhoods} in \Cref{subsection:concrete_tubular_neighbourhood}, and use it to give an explicit formula, \Cref{lemma:differential_of_concrete_tubular_inverse}, for the differential.

\subsection{Outline}
In \Cref{section:stratified_differential_spaces}, we give detailed background on
the geometry of stratified differential spaces.
In \Cref{section:euler_like}, we develop technical tools related to Euler-like vector fields. In \Cref{section:extension_thm_proof}, we prove \Cref{thm:main_diffeomorphism}. In \Cref{section:moser_trick_proof}, we prove \Cref{thm:moser_trick,thm:main_symplectic}. In \Cref{section:coisotropic_theorem}, we prove \Cref{thm:main_coisotropic}. In \Cref{section:exactness_for_isotropic}, we prove that the symplectic form is exact near isotropic stratified subspaces. In \Cref{appendix:strong_deformation_retraction_moser}, we address Moser's trick with a strong deformation retraction, see \Cref{remark:mosers_trick_discussion}.

