\title{Hidden geometric invariance in Laplace-governed magnetization problems}

\author{Yujun Shi}
\email{yujunshi@sxu.edu.cn}
\affiliation{Collage of Physics and Electronic Engineering, Shanxi University, Taiyuan 030006, China}


\begin{abstract}
For over a century, the response of magnetic materials to static fields—formulated as transmission problems of the Laplace equation, has been a standard topic in undergraduate electrodynamics\cite{jackson_classical_1999,Zangwill_2012,griffiths2014introduction,Purcell_Morin_2013,vanderlinde2004classical}. In the infinite permeability limit, the body becomes nearly equipotential and the internal magnetic field nearly vanishes, giving rise to magnetostatic shielding—a phenomenon in which the magnetic induction is nearly zero throughout the interior free space. While this textbook-level result has long been considered fully understood, here, we identify a previously overlooked geometric invariance that complements the quasi-equipotential picture: in the same limit, the entire external magnetic response of a body is determined solely by its outer surface geometry, regardless of internal structure or deformation, as long as the outer surface remains fixed. This surface-encoded invariance encompasses the external field distribution and all magnetic multipole moments of the body, including the dipole and quadrupole, derived as volume integrals from the internal scalar potential. It provides a theoretical foundation for the lightweight design of certain magnetic devices, such as flux concentrators. Given that analogous Laplace transmission problems arise across physics, including electricity conduction, heat conduction and acoustic scattering, this geometric invariance exhibits cross-disciplinary universality. It merits inclusion in undergraduate electromagnetism curricula, as it and the quasi-equipotential property together form a complete characterization of Laplace transmission problems in the infinite permeability limit.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section*{Introduction}\label{sec1}

In essence, both physics and mathematics are concerned with identifying properties or quantities that remain invariant under specific classes of transformations. These invariants form the backbone of our understanding: in physics, they appear as conservation laws and symmetries, ensuring that energy, momentum, and electric charge remain constant despite motion or interaction. In mathematics, they underpin entire branches of geometry and topology—whether it be the distance between points preserved under translation or rotation, the Euler characteristic of a topological surface, or the curvature terms in the Gauss–Bonnet theorem. Invariants not only guide the formulation of natural laws and mathematical classification, but also serve as anchors of thought: Transformations let a problem breathe; Invariants reveal the spirit beneath. Motivated by a pursuit of invariants, this brief paper uncovers a remarkably simple yet robust geometric invariance, overlooked in a textbook-level, well-established problem in classical electromagnetism.

\section*{Transmission problems of the Laplace equation}
The problem concerns the magnetization of a body in an external static magnetic field. Consider a compact (bounded and closed) region \( \overline{\Omega} \subset \mathbb{R}^d (d=2,3) \), which representing a homogeneous, isotropic body with linear constitutive relation \(\mathbf{B}=\mu \mathbf{H}\), embedded in an unbounded medium with permeability \( \mu_0 \). In the absence of free currents, the magnetic field can be expressed as the gradient of a scalar potential \( \phi \), such that \( \mathbf{H} = -\nabla \phi \), and the magnetic induction satisfies \( \mathbf{B} = -\mu \nabla \phi \). The governing equation is then \( \nabla \cdot \mathbf{B} = 0 \), which leads to a classical transmission problem for the Laplace equation. That is, the potential \( \phi \) satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{transmission problem}
	\Delta \phi^- = 0 \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad \Delta \phi^+ = 0 \quad \text{in } \mathbb{R}^d \setminus \overline{\Omega},
\end{equation}
with interface conditions on the boundary \(\Gamma=\partial\Omega\),
\begin{equation}\label{interface conditions}
	\phi^- = \phi^+ \quad \text{on } \Gamma, \qquad
	\mu \frac{\partial \phi^-}{\partial \mathbf{n}} = \mu_0 \frac{\partial \phi^+}{\partial \mathbf{n}} \quad \text{on } \Gamma,
\end{equation}
and an asymptotic condition ensuring that
\begin{equation}\label{asymptotic condition}
	\phi^+(\mathbf{r}) \to\phi^{\mathrm{bg}}(\mathbf{r})  \quad \text{as } |\mathbf{r}| \to \infty,
\end{equation}
where \( \phi^{\mathrm{bg}} \) is a given harmonic background potential defined in the exterior domain, representing the applied magnetic field. Equations~\ref{transmission problem}–\ref{asymptotic condition} together describe how a material body perturbs a background potential field. Beyond magnetostatics, they arise naturally in a broad range of physical contexts, including electrostatics, electricity conduction, steady-state heat conduction, and acoustic scattering.

\section*{Clues to Geometric Invariance}
For a general region \( \Omega \) with arbitrary geometry, analytical solutions of Equations~\ref{transmission problem}–\ref{asymptotic condition} are often unattainable, necessitating the use of numerical approaches such as finite element methods. This naturally raises a fundamental question: do there exist geometric invariances or invariants associated with the region \( \Omega \)? In fact, nontrivial geometric invariances in the context of the Laplace equation are relatively rare. A celebrated example is the conformal invariance of the two-dimensional Laplace equation, which, in principle, allows potential problems in complex geometries to be reformulated in simpler domains. Identifying new invariances under broader classes of geometric transformations remains both challenging and rewarding.


In the infinite permeability limit, the body becomes nearly equipotential and the internal magnetic field \(\mathbf{H}\) nearly vanishes, giving rise to magnetostatic shielding—a phenomenon in which the magnetic induction \(\mathbf{B}\) is nearly zero throughout the interior free space. This quasi-equipotential behavior is a standard description found in nearly all classical electrodynamics textbooks addressing magnetization in the high-permeability limit. While undoubtedly correct, this description is incomplete, as it only concerns the potential field inside the region \( \Omega \). But what about the potential outside, which represents the body's external magnetic response?  Since the zero point of the scalar potential is arbitrary, we can, without loss of generality, shift the reference potential so that the quasi-equipotential corresponds to a quasi-zero potential. In terms of geometric invariance, this quasi-zero potential exhibits a form of "super-invariance", remaining invariant under arbitrary geometric deformations of \( \Omega \). Given that the interior and exterior potential fields are tightly coupled via boundary conditions on the outer surface, the exterior potential field \( \phi^+ \) may likewise exhibit a form of geometric invariance. To the best of our knowledge, this possibility appears to have been overlooked in both textbooks and prior literature.


Further insight into the geometric invariance of \( \phi^+ \) can be gained from the well-understood phenomenon of electrostatic shielding in conductors. The electrostatic response of a conductor to an external field is also governed by the transmission problem for the Laplace equation, albeit with different boundary conditions. Rather than the continuity conditions given by equations~\ref{interface conditions}, the boundary enforces a strict equipotential constraint: 
\begin{equation}\label{equipotential condition}
	\phi^- = \phi^+ \quad \text{on } \Gamma, \qquad
	\phi^-=\text{const.}   \quad \text{in }\Omega,
\end{equation}
which is equivalent to
\begin{equation}\label{limit interface condition}
	\phi^- = \phi^+ \quad \text{on } \Gamma, \qquad
	\frac{\partial \phi^-}{\partial \mathbf{n}} = 0 \quad \text{on } \Gamma.
\end{equation}
From the mathematical structure alone, it is evident that electrostatic shielding represents the limiting case of magnetostatic shielding. Let the conductor’s potential be zero. This exact zero-potential property constitutes a “super-invariance” under arbitrary geometric deformations of the conductor. Moreover, since the external field cannot penetrate the conductor, any alterations to the interior—such as introducing cavities or inserting structures—leave the induced outer surface charge distribution unchanged. This surface charge distribution fully determines the electrostatic response in the exterior region. Hence, the gradient of the potential \( \phi^+ \), corresponding to the electric field distribution outside, is invariant under all internal geometric transformations that preserve the conductor’s exterior surface. Since the conductor has been assigned zero potential, the potential \( \phi^+ \) itself inherits this geometric invariance. However, these invariances are trivial: they are simply a restatement of the classical result that induced surface charge distribution depends solely on the geometry of the conductor’s outer surface. As such, they are often left unstated. Textbook treatments of magnetostatic shielding frequently draw analogies to electrostatic shielding. As illustrated in Fig. 1, discussions of shielding phenomena have traditionally only focused on one side of the coin: an observer inside a hollow shielded region perceives no external field. But what about the other side of the coin—what remains invisible to an external observer?


\begin{figure}[h!]
	\includegraphics[width = 1\textwidth]{Figure01.pdf}
	\caption{\textbf{Electrostatic and magnetostatic shielding viewed from complementary perspectives.}  
		\textbf{a–b}, Classical textbook descriptions of shielding: an observer located inside a hollow, shielded region perceives no external field—electrostatic (\textbf{a}) or magnetostatic (\textbf{b}).  
		\textbf{c}, An often unstated implication: electrostatic shielding renders the external field blind to internal geometric changes, as long as the outer surface remains fixed.  
		\textbf{d}, A subtler but analogous conclusion applies in the magnetostatic case.}

	\label{Figure01}
\end{figure}

In electrostatic shielding, one cannot externally detect any internal geometric modifications that preserve the outer surface of the conductor. But in magnetostatic shielding,  the situation is more subtle. Unlike the purely outer-surface-confined nature of electrostatic induction in conductors, the magnetic response depends on the volumetric distribution of magnetization within the body and is closely tied to its interior geometry. Even from the perspective of equivalent magnetic charges, nonzero magnetic charge distributions arise on both the inner and outer surfaces. Yet, as we will demonstrate, the final conclusion is parallel: an external observer cannot discern any internal geometric modifications that preserve the body's outer surface.

\section*{The Geometric Invariance}
That is, in the limit of infinite permeability, the entire external magnetic response is determined solely by the body's outer surface geometry, regardless of any internal structure or deformation, as long as the outer surface remains fixed. This surface-encoded invariance encompasses the external field distribution and all magnetic multipole moments of the body, including the dipole and quadrupole, derived as volume integrals from the internal scalar potential.  


A complete proof of the above invariance conclusion can be found in the Methods section. The core of the proof can be summarized as follows: in the limit of infinite permeability, let the body approaches a zero-potential. Under any deformation that preserves the outer surface geometry, the solution to the Laplace equation in the exterior region reduces to a Dirichlet exterior problem governed solely by quasi-zero potential prescribed on the fixed outer surface:
\begin{equation}\label{Dirichlet exterior problem}
	\begin{cases}\Delta u(\mathbf{r})=0,&\mathbf{r}\in\mathbb{R}^d\setminus\overline{\Omega}
		\\u(\mathbf{r})\to-\phi^{bg},&\mathbf{r}\in\partial\Omega^{out}
		\\u(\mathbf{r})\to0,&|\mathbf{r}|\to\infty.
	\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where \(u(\mathbf{r})\) is the perturbation term, defined as 
\[\phi^+(\mathbf{r})=u(\mathbf{r})+\phi^{bg}(\mathbf{r}).
\]
The formal solution of the equations \ref{Dirichlet exterior problem} can be composed of the integral of Green function on the boundary,
\begin{equation}\label{external formal solution of potential}
	\begin{aligned} u(\mathbf{r})&=-\int_{\partial\Omega^{out}}dS^{\prime}u(\mathbf{r}^{\prime})\frac{\partial G(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}^{\prime})}{\partial\mathbf{n}^{\prime}} \\&\to\int_{\partial\Omega^{out}}dS^{\prime}\phi^{bg}(\mathbf{r}^{\prime})\frac{\partial G(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}^{\prime})}{\partial\mathbf{n}^{\prime}},
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where \(G(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}^{\prime})\) is the existing and unique Green function, satisfying
\begin{equation}\label{external Green function}
	\begin{cases}
		\Delta G(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}^{\prime})=-\delta(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}^{\prime}),\quad\mathbf{r},\mathbf{r}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^d\setminus\overline{\Omega}\\
		G(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}^{\prime})=0,\quad\mathbf{r}\in\partial\Omega^{out},\mathbf{r}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^d\setminus\overline{\Omega}\\
		G(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}^{\prime})\to0,\quad|\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}^{\prime}|\to\infty,\mathbf{r}\in\partial\Omega^{out},\mathbf{r}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^d\setminus\overline{\Omega}. &
	\end{cases}
\end{equation}

Equation \ref{external formal solution of potential} shows that the solution to the boundary value problem depends continuously on the boundary condition. The resulting scalar potential in the exterior region converges to a unique limiting distribution. Consequently, the magnetic field \(\mathbf{H}\) also approaches a well-defined limiting configuration.  On the other hand, the perturbation term  \(u(\mathbf{r})\) can be expanded in terms of magnetic multipole moments associated with the magnetized body. This immediately implies that, although the internal scalar potential may vary under deformation, the corresponding magnetic multipole moments, expressed as volume integrals, remain invariant. Specifically, the dipole vector can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf{m}=\int_{\Omega}dV^{\prime}\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{r}^{\prime}),
\end{equation}
and the traceless quadrupole tenser is 
\begin{equation}\label{quadrupole tenser 3D}
	Q_{ij}=\int_{\Omega}dV^{\prime}\left[M_{i}(\mathbf{r}^{\prime})r_{j}^{\prime}+M_{j}(\mathbf{r}^{\prime})r_{i}^{\prime}-\frac{2}{3}\delta_{ij}\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{r}^{\prime})\cdot\mathbf{r}^{\prime}\right] \quad\quad (\text{3D}),
\end{equation}
or 
\begin{equation}\label{quadrupole tenser 2D}
	Q_{ij}=\int_{\Omega}dS^{\prime}\left[M_i(\mathbf{r}^{\prime})r_j^{\prime}+M_j(\mathbf{r}^{\prime})r_i^{\prime}-\delta_{ij}\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{r}^{\prime})\cdot\mathbf{r}^{\prime}\right] \quad\quad\quad (\text{2D}).
\end{equation}
And \( \mathbf{M}(\mathbf{r})\) is the magnetization distribution, obtained from 
\[
\mathbf{M} = -(\mu_r-1)\nabla \phi^{-},
\]
where \(\mu_r\) is the relative permeability of the body. Accordingly, \( u(\mathbf{r}) \) admits a magnetic multipole expansion of the form:
\[		u(\mathbf{r})=\quad\frac{1}{4\pi}\left[0+\frac{\mathbf{r}\cdot\mathbf{m}}{r^3}+\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,j}\frac{3r_ir_j}{r^5}Q_{ij}+...\right] \quad\quad (\text{3D}),
\]
or 
\[
u(\mathbf{r})=\quad\frac{1}{2\pi}\left[0+\frac{\mathbf{r}\cdot\mathbf{m}}{r^2}+\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,j}\frac{2r_ir_j}{r^4}Q_{ij}+...\right] \quad\quad (\text{2D}).
\]
Higher-order multipole terms involve increasingly cumbersome expressions and are not presented here for brevity.


\section*{Numerical demonstration of the geometric invariance}

To demonstrate the geometric invariance, we conducted a series of two-dimensional finite element simulations using the \textit{MagnetoDynamics2D} module in \textbf{Elmer}, an open-source platform\cite{elmer}. The computational domain consists of various internal structures sharing a common outer boundary, embedded in vacuum. Figures~\ref{Figure02} and~\ref{Figure03} show the magnetization response under uniform and non-uniform external fields, respectively. In the uniform case (Fig.~\ref{Figure02}), the external flux patterns remain nearly identical across all configurations when the relative permeability is set to $\mu_r = 10^6$ (approximating the infinite limit). The convergence of the magnetic dipole moment and quadrupole tensor with increasing $\mu_r$ is shown in Fig.~\ref{Figure02}d–e, revealing rapid saturation beyond $\mu_r \gtrsim 10^4$. A similar  invariance persists under a spatially varying field generated by a pair of bar magnets (Fig.~\ref{Figure03}).

\begin{figure}[h!]
	\includegraphics[width = 0.8\textwidth]{Figure02.pdf}
	\caption{\textbf{External magnetic response of bodies with identical outer surfaces under a uniform magnetic field.}  
		\textbf{a–c}, Magnetic flux lines and their density in the exterior of three bodies with identical outer surfaces but differing internal structures, each with a relative permeability of $10^6$ (approximating the infinite-permeability limit): a solid body (\textbf{a}), a hollow shell (\textbf{b}), and a thin shell (\textbf{c}).  
		\textbf{d–e}, Magnetic dipole vector components (\textbf{d}) and quadrupole tensor components (\textbf{e}) as functions of increasing permeability. All values are normalized to those of the solid body at relative permeability $10^6$. Note that a symmetric and traceless second-order tensor \(\mathbf{Q}\) in two dimensions has only two independent degrees of freedom.}

	\label{Figure02}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h!]
	\includegraphics[width = 0.8\textwidth]{Figure03.pdf}

	\caption{\textbf{External magnetic response of bodies with identical outer surfaces under a nonuniform magnetic field.}
		\textbf{a,e}, A nonuniform magnetic field generated by a pair of uniformly magnetized bar magnets.
		\textbf{b–d}, Magnetic flux lines and their density in the exterior of three bodies with identical outer surfaces but differing internal structures, each with a relative permeability of $10^6$ (approximating the infinite-permeability limit): a solid body (\textbf{b}), a hollow shell (\textbf{c}), and a thin shell (\textbf{d}).
		\textbf{f–g}, Magnetic dipole vector components (\textbf{f}) and quadrupole tensor components (\textbf{g}) as functions of increasing permeability. All values are normalized to those of the solid body at a relative permeability of $10^6$.}
	\label{Figure03}
\end{figure}


\section*{Application to Lightweight Device Design}

One of the most immediate applications of the geometric invariance identified in this work lies in enabling lightweight designs of magnetic devices. A representative example is the magnetic flux concentrator (MFC), which is widely employed in magnetic sensors, energy harvesters, and wireless power transfer systems\cite{10.1063/1.3056152,PhysRevResearch.2.023394,D0EE01574A,10265057}. Conventionally, MFCs are designed as fully solid structures composed of soft magnetic materials with high relative permeability—ranging from $\mu_r \sim 10^2$–$10^4$ for ferrites to $\mu_r \sim 10^4$–$10^5$ for mu-metal. The invariance observed here provides a clear insight: when the device’s functionality depends solely on its external magnetic response, a hollow structure can serve as an effective surrogate for its solid counterpart.  And such hollowing strategies have been rarely explored in the existing literature on MFC design. 

Figure~\ref{Figure04} illustrates this concept by comparing the magnetic response of solid (100\% volume fraction) and thin-shell (8.7\%) MFC structures under a uniform external field. At a high permeability of $\mu_r = 10^4$, the two configurations exhibit virtually identical performance, underscoring the potential for significant reductions in material usage and device weight without compromising functionality.  




\begin{figure}[h!]
	\includegraphics[width = 0.8\textwidth]{Figure04.pdf}
	\caption{\textbf{Magnetic flux concentration in solid and hollow MFC structures under a uniform external field.} 
		\textbf{a–d}, Magnetic flux lines and their density in the exterior of two magnetic flux concentrators (MFCs), each with a relative permeability of $10^4$: solid MFC with 100\% volume fraction (\textbf{a,~b}), and hollow MFC with 8.7\% volume fraction (\textbf{c,~d}). Panels \textbf{b} and \textbf{d} show magnified views of the central regions in \textbf{a} and \textbf{c}, respectively.  
		\textbf{e}, Relative magnetic flux density along the central axis (indicated by the white arrow in \textbf{b}) of the hollow MFC, normalized to that of the solid MFC. Inset: absolute magnetic flux density along the central axes of both structures.}

